window.EXPLAIN={"de55ec71":["B6",["Find the subject: \"Generations of mystery and horror writers\".","Find its verb: \"have been influenced\", which comes right after the blank.","The blank sits between the subject and its verb, and nothing can go there. Pick the choice with no punctuation."],"The long subject makes you want to pause, but a breath isn't a comma."],"c3397d25":["B3",["Read what comes before the blank: \"Egyptologists have commonly divided ancient Egyptian history into three primary periods\". Could it stand alone? Yes, it's a full sentence.","What comes after is a list of the three periods (Old, Middle, New Kingdom), and it has no verb of its own.","Full sentence + list → colon. A period or semicolon would turn the list into a fragment."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"the Old Kingdom…, the Middle Kingdom…, and the New Kingdom…\" as a fragment with no verb."],"e38b3e4f":["F1",["Find the verb's subject. Cross out the extra stuff: \"The radiation [that ___ during the decay of radioactive atomic nuclei]\".","The thing doing the occurring is \"radiation\", which is singular (one thing). \"Nuclei\" is just in a describing phrase.","Singular subject → singular verb: \"occurs\"."],"\"Nuclei\" sits right before the verb and is plural, but it's not the subject."],"89fbc3eb":["B1",["Cover the blank. Left side: \"The Mission 66 initiative... represented a major investment in the infrastructure of overburdened national parks\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"it prioritized physical improvements to the parks' roads...\" \"It\" + \"prioritized\" is a subject and a verb, so it's a full sentence too.","Two full sentences → period, semicolon, or comma + FANBOYS. The semicolon is the only choice that does it."],"D is a comma splice. A is a run-on-ish \"and\" with no comma, and B smashes them together."],"960dec02":["B5",["Look at \"Entomologist Heather Grab\". \"Entomologist\" is a title stuck right onto her name, like \"Doctor Smith\".","A title stuck to a name is essential, so it gets no commas. Also, \"Entomologist Heather Grab\" is the subject of \"found\".","No comma between the title and the name, and no comma between the subject and its verb → no punctuation at all."],"Commas around the name only work for extra info like \"the entomologist, Heather Grab, found…\""],"37e5c794":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Despite being cheap, versatile, and easy to produce\".","Ask what is cheap, versatile, and easy to produce. Commercial plastics.","So \"commercial plastics\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with it."],"A starts with \"there,\" B starts with \"two problems,\" and C starts with \"plastics' problems.\" Problems aren't cheap and easy to produce."],"4b0c7b62":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"The reed of a wind instrument is the mouthpiece where sound is made.\"","Is it asking something? No. It's telling you what the reed is. \"Where sound is made\" just describes the mouthpiece.","Statement → normal word order (\"sound is made\") + period."],"\"Where\" looks like a question word, but here it just describes the mouthpiece."],"6f08641e":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"After pleading for years—to no effect—for the passage of key antidiscrimination legislation\".","Who did the pleading? The activists (Kitty Cone and the others).","So \"the activists\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with it."],"Lawmakers, pressure, and a protest didn't do the pleading. Only D puts the activists first."],"3a1239d2":["F4",["Find the sentence's main verb. Read on past the blank: \"scientists... adapted the test\". \"Adapted\" is the main verb.","The sentence already has its verb, so the blank can't be a second one. It must be part of a describing phrase about the scientists.","Describing phrase → -ing form: \"scientists searching for evidence... adapted the test.\""],"\"Searched\" or \"were searching\" would give \"scientists\" two main verbs with no \"and\" between them."],"3580533b":["F3",["Find the time clues: the sentence before says economists \"use\" the tool. That's present tense.","This sentence states a general fact about what the tool does.","Present facts → simple present: \"measures\"."],"\"Had measured,\" \"would have measured,\" and \"will have been measuring\" all point to some other time that the passage never mentions."],"2c49940e":["F6",["How many phrases? One (\"I think, therefore I am\"). Does it own something? Yes, its simplicity → \"phrase's\".","The second blank refers back to that one phrase → singular pronoun.","\"Phrase's simplicity masks its complexity\". It's singular both times."],"\"Phrases'\" would mean more than one phrase, but there's only \"I think, therefore I am.\""],"e62241b7":["F1",["Find the subject: \"the country's name\".","\"Name\" is one thing, so it's singular. \"Country's\" just says whose name it is.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Country's\" isn't the subject. It just tells you whose name."],"7f226b4b":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"the cellular organs that generate energy from light\". It explains what chloroplasts are.","Find its other end. It already ends with a dash: \"...from light—reshuffled\".","It ends with a dash, so it must start with a dash. Matching pair → \"chloroplasts—\"."],"A comma would open the aside with a comma and close it with a dash, which isn't a matching pair."],"74ce2f05":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"carbon-13 (13 C)\". It names the isotope.","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"isotope\", so it must end with a comma after \"(13 C)\".","Delete test: \"a rare carbon isotope, due to the higher levels in corn...\" still works → \"carbon-13 (13 C),\"."],"C adds a comma between \"carbon-13\" and its own abbreviation, which splits a name from its label."],"a2816c7f":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read past the blank: \"Serra... assembles large-scale steel plates\". \"Assembles\" is the main verb.","The blank can't be a second main verb, so it starts an aside about Serra that ends at the comma after \"environment\".","Aside → comma + -ing form: \"Serra, intending his installations to...\"."],"\"Serra intends\" would give the sentence two main verbs. \"Serra, intends\" puts a comma between the subject and the verb."],"a7c85001":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"Researchers... investigated how people perceive acts of kindness.\"","Is it asking? No. It's telling you what they investigated. The question is only being described.","Statement → normal word order (\"people perceive\") + period."],"A question mark only goes on an actual question like \"How do people perceive…?\""],"eb95235b":["B1",["Cover the blank. Left side: \"The Limón technique... is known for its emphasis on breath control and its interplay of weight and weightlessness\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"dancers may explore, for example, the moment of mid-air suspension...\" It's a full sentence too.","Two full sentences → semicolon."],"C is a comma splice. B's \"which\" makes it nonsense."],"adf210e7":["B2",["Left side: \"The haiku-like poems... have earned the Swedish poet praise from leading contemporary writers\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"among them Nigerian American essayist... Teju Cole, who has written...\" Is there a main verb? No (\"has written\" belongs to \"who\"). It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"Among them… Teju Cole…\" standing alone as a fragment."],"b7363ba2":["F6",["Who owns the wings? The butterfly, which is \"it\". Owned by it → \"its\" (no apostrophe). \"It's\" always means \"it is\".","Do the wings own anything? No, they're just plural → \"wings\".","\"its wings\""],"\"It's\" always means \"it is.\" \"Wings'\" would mean the wings own something."],"908a76b8":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? Go back to \"a given industry\".","\"Industry\" is singular. The examples (water and electricity) are inside dashes, so they're just an aside.","Singular → \"it often consists\"."],"The examples \"water and electricity\" sit in the middle and make it feel plural, but they're inside dashes, so they're just an aside."],"707461d8":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2021, ... Macías-Rubalcava led a review of the scientific literature related to endophytic fungi (...)\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"By researching 120 ... compounds, she found that...\" \"She found\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period after the parenthesis: \"plant). By\"."],"B is a comma splice. The parentheses distract you, but you still need a boundary after them."],"1ee7b429":["F3",["Find the time clues: the other verbs are \"made\" and \"collected\", which are past.","She was in the middle of studying when she made the discovery.","Past + ongoing → \"was studying\"."],"\"Studies,\" \"has been studying,\" and \"will study\" break the past-tense story."],"13fcf575":["B5",["Look at what follows the blank: \"that it has become known as 'Maracaibo's Lighthouse'\".","It finishes the \"so dependable...\" idea, so it's essential. Delete it and \"so dependable\" has nothing to complete.","Essential \"that\" clause → no punctuation."],"\"That\" clauses almost never get a comma, colon, or semicolon before them."],"940ff6f7":["B1",["Left side: \"Jamaican British artist Willard Wigan is known for his remarkable microsculptures\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Creations so small that..., Wigan's sculptures are made from tiny natural materials\". \"Sculptures are made\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period."],"B is a comma splice. The description \"creations so small…\" belongs to the second sentence, describing the sculptures."],"333b2b65":["B2",["Left side: \"While one requires oxygen and one does not\". It starts with \"While\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Right side: \"aerobic and anaerobic respiration are both forms of cellular respiration\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"While one… does not\" as a fragment."],"81f43b85":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"This has not always been the case\" and \"throughout geologic history, Earth's magnetic poles have swapped places\".","\"Though\" means \"however\" here and goes with the first one: \"This has not always been the case, though.\"","Put the semicolon after \"though\", at the real sentence boundary: \"case, though;\"."],"In C the semicolon goes before \"though,\" which glues \"though\" onto the second sentence and wrecks the meaning."],"4c335aea":["F6",["Do the genres own something? Yes, the chorus-to-verse ratios belong to rock and rap.","Each genre is singular (one rock, one rap) → singular possessive for each.","\"rock's and rap's\""],"\"Rocks\" and \"raps\" would be plurals, as if there were several rocks, with no ownership."],"8b017d4e":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Supported by biochemical analyses of over 2,000 skeletons from the Middle Ages\".","What would be supported by analyses? Findings from a study.","So \"findings\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with it."],"C reads naturally but starts with \"the primary components,\" which weren't supported by analyses."],"9127635a":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read past the blank: \"Udé... honors its legacy\". \"Honors\" is the main verb.","So the blank begins an aside about Udé (\"having referred to Nollywood... as...\") that ends at the comma after \"excellence\".","Aside → comma + \"having\": \"Udé, having\"."],"\"Udé has\" or \"Udé, has\" would give the sentence two main verbs fighting over one subject."],"aaa1907f":["B7",["Spot the list of three things Belpré did: storytelling...; celebrated...; put on puppet shows.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes (\"in both English and Spanish, an uncommon practice\"). And there's a \"; and\" later.","Complex list → semicolons between items. \"An uncommon practice at the time\" stays attached to item 1 → \"practice at the time;\"."],"C and D use commas, which can't separate complex list items once the list is already using semicolons."],"7f48b098":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"the mechanism by which plants use sunlight to turn carbon dioxide and water into nutrients\".","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"Photosynthesis\".","Opens with a comma → closes with a comma. Delete test: \"Photosynthesis is fueled in part by...\" ✓ → \"nutrients,\"."],"A dash would open with a comma and close with a dash, which doesn't match. No punctuation would leave the aside unclosed."],"626a1642":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"which assume that people are consistently rational decision-makers\".","Find its other end. It ends with a dash: \"decision-makers—cannot explain\".","Ends with a dash → starts with a dash. Delete test: \"anomalies that neoclassical economic models cannot explain\" ✓ → \"models—which\"."],"B opens with a comma and closes with a dash, which doesn't match."],"36e89f74":["F4",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"a pattern of fracturing in rocks that ___ in parallel arrays\".","A \"that\" clause needs a real (tensed) verb. Its subject is \"pattern\", which is singular.","Real, singular verb → \"results\"."],"\"Resulting\" and \"to result\" aren't real verbs. \"Were\" is plural."],"035fd57e":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"Currently\".","\"Currently\" means now.","Now → present → \"includes\"."],"Past and future forms clash with \"currently.\""],"148be4da":["B1",["Left side: \"Human-made (synthetic) fibers... are more durable than most natural plant fibers\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"the manufacture of synthetic fibers requires toxic chemical solvents...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences with no semicolon option → comma + FANBOYS: \"fibers, but\"."],"A is a comma splice. B has \"but\" with no comma."],"9df6da04":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Knowing that flying insects keep their backs pointed toward sunlight during the day\".","Who knows that? The researchers.","So \"the researchers\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with it."],"B starts with \"the researchers' theory,\" and theories don't know things. C and D start with \"moths.\""],"0f39b19c":["B1",["Left side: \"Defying all odds, Rudolph didn't just walk, she ran—fast\". It's a full sentence (the dash before \"fast\" is a single dash for emphasis).","Right side: \"During the 1960 Summer Olympics in Rome, she won both the 100- and 200-meter dashes...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"ran—fast. During\"."],"The dash before \"fast\" is a single dash adding emphasis. It doesn't need a partner."],"ae439895":["F1",["Find the verb's subject. Cross out \"a person has internalized (class, gender, political affiliation, etc.)\".","What's left: \"the various cultural and ideological frameworks ___ each emotional reaction\". \"Frameworks\" is plural.","Plural → \"underlie\"."],"\"Person,\" \"affiliation,\" and \"etc.\" are all closer to the verb but aren't the subject."],"83617355":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"is bathed\" and \"The source of the glow is\" are present.","The sculpture still exists, and the passage describes it now.","Present → \"illuminates\"."],"\"Illuminated,\" \"would illuminate,\" and \"will be illuminating\" shift the time for no reason."],"16740ab4":["F1",["Find the verb's subject. Cross out \"of many fossilized microbacteria, which seem to have thrived there despite...\".","What's left: \"the presence ___ claims\". \"Presence\" is singular.","Singular → \"reinforces\"."],"\"Microbacteria\" is plural and right before the verb. That's the trap."],"f78997cf":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Such interpretations are ultimately speculative\" and \"the object's gray coloration may be an incidental effect...\".","\"Though\" means \"however\" here and goes with the first one: \"...speculative, though.\"","Semicolon after \"though\", at the real boundary: \"speculative, though;\"."],"C puts the semicolon before \"though,\" attaching it to the wrong sentence."],"f0864217":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Based on events that occurred when Rabinal was a city-state ruled by a king\".","What is based on events? The drama, Rabinal Achí.","So \"Rabinal Achí\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with it."],"B and C start with the military leader. A person isn't \"based on events.\""],"c91ef0f0":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Amplifying the voices and experiences of Black soldiers\".","An -ing action used as a subject counts as one thing, so it's singular.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Voices,\" \"experiences,\" and \"soldiers\" are plural, but they're objects inside the subject."],"7b419faf":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"guided\" (1903) and \"upheld\" are past.","The vow is part of the same past story.","Past → \"vowed\"."],"Present and future forms break the past-tense story."],"29c9be28":["F4",["Find the subject: \"embryos inside African turquoise killifish eggs\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb for \"embryos\". \"-ing\", \"to ___\", and \"having ___\" forms don't count.","Real verb → \"enter\"."],"-ing and \"to ___\" choices are the classic trap when a sentence has no verb yet."],"983d33fa":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Some historians\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb for \"historians\".","Real verb → \"claim\"."],"\"Claiming,\" \"having claimed,\" and \"to claim\" leave the sentence without a main verb."],"6e193b19":["F1",["What does \"which\" refer to? \"the Nerf football\", which is one football.","It's singular, and it still exists, so present tense.","Singular + present → \"is a smaller, foam version\"."],"\"Were\" and \"are\" are plural. The football is singular."],"fced396a":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Compared with that of a highly concentrated market\". \"That of\" means \"the HHI score of\".","So you're comparing scores. The thing after the comma has to be a score.","\"the HHI score of a less concentrated market\" is the only choice that starts with a score."],"A, C, and D start with a market or \"when,\" which compares a market to a score."],"e6f2dba6":["F4",["Find the subject: \"yellow\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb for \"yellow\".","Real verb → \"is\"."],"\"Being,\" \"to be,\" and \"having been\" leave the sentence without a main verb."],"52b61716":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"By the end of the 1990s\", a deadline in the past.","The doubling was finished before that past deadline.","Finished before a past point → \"had doubled\"."],"\"Has doubled\" is for something that continues into now. Present and future don't fit the 1990s."],"4dcedc31":["B5",["Look at \"chemistry professor Yugang Sun\". The title \"professor\" is stuck right onto the name.","A title stuck to a name is essential, so no commas. And \"professor Yugang Sun and his colleagues\" is the subject of \"developed\".","No punctuation → \"professor Yugang Sun\"."],"Commas around the name only work with \"the\" or \"a\" before the title (\"a professor, Yugang Sun, …\")."],"96c720af":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"travel\" and \"are diverted\" are present.","The sentence describes how atoms always behave.","Present → \"reach\"."],"\"Will reach\" and \"had reached\" break the present-tense pattern."],"dbd78791":["F3",["Look at the pattern: \"the Sherpas pack up their telescopes and ___ ready\".","The two verbs share \"the Sherpas\" and must match \"pack\", which is present.","Matching present → \"get\"."],"\"Got\" and \"were getting\" switch to the past in the middle of a present-tense routine."],"819c443d":["F6",["How many authors? One (Paine). Does he own something? Yes, the writings → \"author's\".","Do the writings own anything? No → plain plural \"writings\".","\"author's political writings\""],"C makes \"writing's\" possessive for no reason. D makes \"authors'\" plural, as if there were several authors."],"100269ad":["F1",["Find the subject: \"The vest frottoir\".","One instrument → singular.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Have been,\" \"were,\" and \"are\" are all plural."],"674e59a3":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"By 2023\", a deadline in the past.","She became famous before that point.","Finished before a past point → \"had become\"."],"\"Will have become\" and \"will become\" point to the future, but 2023 is the past in this passage."],"0aebdf5f":["F1",["Find the subject: \"speakers (of the English language)\".","\"Speakers\" is plural. \"Language\" is just in a describing phrase.","Plural → \"use\"."],"\"Language\" is singular and right before the verb, but it isn't the subject."],"f10b7ce4":["F1",["Find the subject: \"her book's musings (on tenth-century Japanese courtly life)\".","\"Musings\" is plural.","Plural → \"fascinate\"."],"\"Book\" and \"life\" are singular and closer to the verb. That's the trap."],"155239cf":["B1",["Left side: \"Scientists had previously thought the Sun to be in a relatively 'quiet' phase at that time\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"the Lund team's finding suggests otherwise\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"time, but\"."],"A has \"but\" with no comma. C is a comma splice. D is a run-on."],"82a76537":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"for instance\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It already ends with a comma: \"instance, you could\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma: \"newts, for instance,\"."],"A period or semicolon before \"for\" would leave \"In a JSON file storing data about fire belly newts\" as a fragment."],"e2f77ae7":["B7",["Spot the list of three continuations: The Siege of Thebes...; The Tale of Beryn...; The Ploughman's Tale....","Do the items have commas inside? Yes (\"The Siege of Thebes, which purports...\"). And there's a \"; and\" later.","Complex list → semicolon between items: \"return;\"."],"A comma would blur where one title's description ends and the next title begins."],"9091458d":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"How are they able to move so fast?\"","Is it really asking? Yes. The passage asks it and then answers it in the next sentence.","Real question → question order (\"are they\") + question mark."],"A and C use statement order. B has question order with a period."],"ac5536c1":["B2",["Left side: \"...but she also dedicated herself to mycology, the study of fungi\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"producing more than 350 paintings... and submitting her research...\" It has no subject + main verb, just -ing words. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"fungi, producing\"."],"A semicolon or period would leave \"Producing… and submitting…\" as a fragment."],"5bed774c":["B1",["Left side: \"Philosopher Peter Kivy was a leading figure in musical aesthetics\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"As evidenced by his belief that..., Kivy was a formalist...\" It has its own subject and verb (\"Kivy was\"), so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period."],"C is a comma splice. The long \"as evidenced…\" intro makes it hard to see where the second sentence's main verb is."],"4aa28ac3":["F4",["Look at the verb before the blank: \"the Olympic committee once decided ___\".","After \"decided\", English uses \"to + verb\" (\"decided to go\", \"decided to include\").","\"to include\""],"\"Included\" would give two main verbs. You can't say \"decided including.\""],"9c3630b9":["B7",["Spot the list of three kinds of materials: natural debris (such as dried leaves); man-made trash (such as plastic bags); and art supplies (such as tree glue).","Do the items have commas inside? Yes (\"natural debris, such as dried leaves\"). And there's a \"; and\" later.","Semicolon between items, comma before \"such as\" inside an item → \"leaves; man-made trash,\"."],"C uses commas everywhere, so the items blur together. D puts a semicolon in the middle of an item."],"8df848c1":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? \"the eating utensils\" (the table forks).","\"Utensils\" is plural.","Plural → \"them\"."],"\"This,\" \"that,\" and \"it\" are singular."],"77bf77cd":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out the aside \", a geologist and space scientist,\".","What's left: \"Farouk El-Baz ___ part of the team\". That's one person, so singular.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Are,\" \"have been,\" and \"were\" are plural."],"74253458":["F2",["What does the blank point to? \"plants\" (the cycads).","\"Plants\" is plural.","Plural pointer → \"These\"."],"\"That\" and \"This\" are singular. \"Each\" would need \"plant.\""],"3a2d77d7":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"novel audio technologies allowed the addition of another instrument to jazz and swing music\". It's a full sentence.","What follows names that instrument: \"the flute\".","Full sentence + name → colon. Then \"A relatively quiet instrument, its full range...\" starts a new sentence → \"music: the flute. A\"."],"B and C leave \"The flute, a relatively quiet instrument, its full range…\" all crammed together with no main verb for \"the flute.\""],"435809d8":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"The vessel took six days to dislodge, in part due to its sheer size\". It's a full sentence (the \"in part...\" add-on is set off by a comma).","What follows explains it: \"it's as heavy as two thousand blue whales\". That's another full sentence that explains the first.","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"dislodge, in part due to its sheer size:\"."],"C puts a comma before a full sentence (\"it's as heavy…\"), which is a comma splice."],"ea0aa676":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of plants in Silent Valley, a pristine tropical forest... (many of which are endangered)\".","What's left: \"Her exhaustive chromosomal survey ___ instrumental\". \"Survey\" is singular.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Species,\" \"flora,\" and \"many\" are all plural and sit closer to the blank."],"353890a1":["F4",["Look at the verb before the blank: \"the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration seeks ___\".","After \"seeks\", English uses \"to + verb\".","\"to expand\""],"The other choices are real verbs, which would give the sentence two main verbs."],"b0115ef6":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"...led a team... to investigate how their use could be better regulated.\"","Is it asking? No. It's describing what the team investigated.","Statement → normal order (\"their use could be\") + period."],"A and D use question order. C uses a question mark on a statement."],"ee4aa2aa":["F1",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"coiled branching structures that ___ it survive\".","The \"that\" refers to \"structures\", which is plural.","Plural, real verb → \"help\"."],"\"The species\" is singular and sounds like the subject, but the \"that\" clause describes the structures."],"83898524":["B4",["Spot the extra info: the list \"New Granada (...), Venezuela, and Quito (present-day Ecuador)\".","Find its other end. It starts with a dash after \"territories—\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. Close the parenthesis first: \"Ecuador)—\"."],"A comma would open with a dash and close with a comma, which doesn't match."],"fba5d8d1":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"there was one personality trait that they scored particularly high on\". It's a full sentence.","What follows names that trait: \"openness to experience\".","Full sentence + name → colon: \"high on:\"."],"A period would leave \"On openness to experience\" as a fragment."],"57998dd3":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"in a later chapter\".","\"Later\" points to the future.","Future → \"will learn\"."],"\"Had learned\" and \"have learned\" talk about the past."],"dc645172":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", most known for her 1979 historical novel Sally Hemings and the conversation it inspired,\".","What's left: \"The artistic talents of Barbara Chase-Riboud ___ limited\". \"Talents\" is plural.","Plural → \"aren't\"."],"Long asides (the novel, the conversation) push the verb away, and \"Barbara Chase-Riboud\" is singular."],"6fece68e":["B7",["Spot the list of three things the Law established: the sanctity of animal life / the just treatment of the elderly / the abolition of the slave trade.","Do the items have commas inside? No, it's a simple list.","Simple list → commas: \"life,\"."],"Semicolons are only for lists whose items already have commas inside."],"886dc9f9":["B1",["Left side: \"On July 23, 1854, a clipper ship called the Flying Cloud entered San Francisco Bay\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Having left New York Harbor..., the celebrated ship set a record...\" \"The ship set\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"Bay. Having\"."],"C makes a comma splice, because \"Having left…\" belongs to the second sentence's subject, \"the ship.\""],"166efaa2":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? \"customers\".","\"Customers\" is plural.","Plural → \"they\"."],"\"One,\" \"you,\" and \"it\" don't match a plural group of customers."],"59a246dc":["B5",["Look at \"the chemical compound aluminum oxide\".","Is \"aluminum oxide\" essential? Yes. Without it you don't know which compound.","Essential name → no commas: \"compound aluminum oxide\"."],"Commas around the name would treat it as removable, but then the reader wouldn't know which compound."],"a153ad6a":["B1",["Left side: \"One such type of matter is a form of cooled, condensed gas called a Bose-Einstein condensate (BEC)\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Dutch physicist Lene Hau famously used a BEC to slow a beam of light...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"(BEC);\"."],"A is a comma splice and D is a run-on. The parentheses distract you."],"e060dd6b":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Recordings of electrical activity in the brain\".","What is a recording of brain activity? Electrograms.","So \"electrograms\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with it."],"B, C, and D start with players, responses, or scenarios, which aren't recordings."],"35ae047d":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Land\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"used\"."],"\"To have used,\" \"to use,\" and \"using\" can't be the main verb."],"9eb43963":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What swirls into a whirlpool? \"Water\".","\"Water\" is singular (one mass).","Singular → \"it\"."],"\"Winds\" and \"landmasses\" are plural, but they're not what swirls."],"5ef9fc48":["B5",["Look at what follows the blank: \"that some biologists believe can be used to explain...\". It describes the framework.","It's essential because it tells you which framework. And \"Some biologists believe can be used\" isn't a full sentence on its own.","Essential \"that\" clause → no punctuation: \"formation that some\"."],"A puts a comma before \"that.\" B and D treat \"Some biologists believe can be used…\" as a sentence, but it's missing a subject."],"eeb14722":["F6",["Who owns the protagonists? The film, which is \"it\". Owned by it → \"its\" (no apostrophe).","How many protagonists? Two, and they don't own anything → plain plural \"protagonists\".","\"its two protagonists\""],"\"Protagonist's\" with an apostrophe means one protagonist owns something."],"6e071432":["B4",["Spot the extra info. It starts with a dash after \"photographs\": \"—both of interviewees and the items they described, from hair to grass to sculptures\".","Find its other end. It hasn't been closed yet, and the sentence continues with \"to powerful effect\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. The comma before \"from\" sets off the examples → \"described, from hair to grass to sculptures—\"."],"C closes the aside with a comma, which doesn't match its opening dash."],"db4e3819":["F1",["Find the subject: \"she\" (Alice Coltrane).","\"She\" is singular, and the other verb is \"creates\" (present).","Singular present → \"sweeps\"."],"\"Sweep,\" \"are sweeping,\" and \"were sweeping\" don't go with \"she.\""],"20ea68b7":["F6",["Does anything own anything? No. It's \"the number of terms presidents can serve\".","Terms: more than one, plural. Presidents: more than one, plural.","Plain plurals, no apostrophes → \"terms presidents\"."],"Apostrophes (\"term's,\" \"president's\") are a trap when nothing is doing any owning."],"77e06a09":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"through trade and centralized taxation\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"that\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"a complex economic system that funded major infrastructure projects\" ✓ → \"taxation,\"."],"A colon or dash wouldn't match the opening comma."],"6ea8c23f":["B6",["Find the core: \"the team was able to determine [how mixing varies across regions] and [what impact it has]\".","Those are two objects of \"determine\" joined by \"and\". They're not two sentences.","Don't split a verb from its objects, and don't put a comma before \"and\" when it joins two objects → no punctuation."],"\"And\" only needs a comma when it joins two full sentences."],"aab74a3b":["B1",["Left side: \"Researcher Lin Zhi developed a process for increasing the tensile strength... of silkworm silk\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"By dissolving and reweaving the silk..., Zhi increased the amount of force...\" \"Zhi increased\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"silk. By\"."],"A is a comma splice. \"By…\" looks like a fragment, but it's the intro to a full second sentence."],"1724dac2":["B1",["Left side: \"A subseasonal weather forecast attempts to predict weather conditions three to four weeks in advance\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"its predictions are therefore more short-term...\". It's a full sentence (\"therefore\" can't join anything).","Two full sentences → semicolon."],"A is a comma splice. \"Therefore\" sits inside the second sentence and can't join them on its own."],"512f0ac9":["F4",["Find the main verbs: \"re-created and then reprogrammed\". The sentence already has them.","The blank explains why they did it (the purpose).","Purpose → \"to forge\"."],"\"Forged\" would be a third main verb with no \"and.\""],"4320b4ad":["F4",["Find the subject: \"the women who had worked for the US military...\". Is there a main verb yet? No (\"had worked\" belongs to \"who\").","The sentence needs a real verb for \"the women\".","Real verb → \"kept\"."],"\"To keep,\" \"having kept,\" and \"keeping\" aren't main verbs."],"e67f9967":["B2",["Left side: \"For I Am Somebody, ... director Madeline Anderson chose a narrator who had participated in the protest\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"a decision that, by allowing..., amplified...\" The verb belongs to \"that\", so there's no main verb. It's a fragment. \"By allowing... their own\" is an aside inside it.","Sentence + fragment → comma. The aside needs a matching comma after \"that\" → \"protest, a decision that,\"."],"A and B would leave \"A decision that…\" standing alone as a fragment."],"1aa3f174":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"(as many as 200,000, by some estimates\". It opened with \"(\".","Find its other end. It has to close with \")\".","Close the parenthesis. Then \"housed its residents in a complex\" needs no comma → \"estimates)\"."],"B adds a comma that splits \"housed its residents\" from \"in a complex.\""],"a9e5b788":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Saville is not the protagonist of Shelley's novel\" and \"as the recipient of the letters..., she's the 'occasion' of it\".","\"Rather\" is a transition word, not FANBOYS, so it can't join sentences.","Semicolon at the boundary, then \"rather,\" → \"novel; rather,\"."],"D tries to join two sentences with \", rather,\" which is still a comma splice."],"cdbbbf94":["B6",["Find the core: \"an extra leap second is added [whenever time... lags]\".","The \"whenever\" clause comes after the main sentence and finishes its meaning, so it can't stand alone.","A when/whenever/because clause at the end gets no punctuation → \"added whenever\"."],"A semicolon or period would leave \"Whenever time… lags…\" as a fragment."],"a3e87535":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"a fictionalized account of the lives of the Mirabal sisters\".","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after the title \"Butterflies\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"Julia Alvarez's 1994 novel... can serve as a starting point\" ✓ → \"sisters,\"."],"No comma would leave the aside unclosed. \"And\" adds a second verb that doesn't fit."],"d47bb0a4":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"ranging from the Kikkoman soy sauce bottle to... the Komachi bullet train\".","What's left: \"Objects ___ designed\". \"Objects\" is plural.","Plural → \"were\"."],"The examples are each singular, which pulls you toward \"was.\""],"e3b72630":["F4",["Find the main verb: \"Margarita Engle uses poetry\". The sentence already has its verb.","The blank explains why she uses poetry (the purpose).","Purpose → \"to tell\"."],"\"Tells,\" \"told,\" and \"is telling\" would be a second main verb."],"0560b2b8":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What uses horror tropes? \"dark fiction\".","\"Dark fiction\" is singular.","Singular → \"it uses\"."],"\"Readers\" and \"Jones\" are nearby, but the sentence is about what dark fiction does."],"d2b81427":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"In assessing the films of Japanese director Akira Kurosawa\".","Who assesses films? Critics.","So \"many critics\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with it."],"B starts with \"Kurosawa's use…,\" and a use can't assess anything. C starts with \"there are.\""],"56315bd0":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"that absorbs sustainable energies, redistributing them through intricate networks of weights and generators\".","What's left: \"an artificial intelligence ___ Chicago's energy grid\". One AI, so singular.","Singular → \"powers\"."],"\"Energies,\" \"networks,\" \"weights,\" and \"generators\" are all plural and nearby."],"ffea47e1":["B6",["Find the core: \"deposited during the Late Cretaceous\". \"During\" is a preposition and \"the Late Cretaceous\" is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"during\"."],"Any mark after \"during\" cuts the phrase in half."],"a1e0c981":["B6",["Find the core: \"themes of childhood, womanhood, and Chinese American identity\". \"Of\" is a preposition and the list is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object, even when the object is a list.","No punctuation → \"of\"."],"A colon looks like it introduces the list, but colons need a full sentence before them, and \"examines themes of\" isn't one."],"b35cefb7":["B2",["Left side: \"Because regolith is both readily available and high in oxygen content\". It starts with \"Because\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Right side: \"scientists have wondered whether it could be used...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"content,\"."],"D's \", and\" would join a fragment to a sentence as if both were full sentences."],"e76e74e8":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"The number of jams available for tasting varied\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains how it varied: \"some shoppers had twenty-four different options, others only six\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"varied:\"."],"B is a comma splice. C and D use \"while,\" which turns the explanation into a contrast."],"b74f676f":["F4",["Find the pattern: \"Price premiered her First Symphony—[aside]—and ___ supportive relationships\".","Skip the dashes. The blank pairs with \"premiered\" through \"and\", so it needs the same form.","Match \"premiered\" → \"developed\"."],"The dashes make you lose track of \"premiered.\" Skip the aside and the pattern is obvious."],"1a61e2ae":["B4",["Find the main verb. Read past the blank: \"John Donne's works... had... fallen into relative obscurity\". \"Had\" is the main verb.","So \"much admired during his lifetime... and in the decades that followed\" is an aside. It ends with a comma after \"followed\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma, and no extra verb: \"works,\"."],"\"Works were\" or \"works had been\" gives the subject two verbs (\"were… had\")."],"2d6f8304":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"since 1945\".","\"Since\" means from then until now.","Then until now → \"has collected\"."],"\"Collects\" or \"is collecting\" ignores the \"since…\" span."],"3a35ddd1":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"is unable\" and \"helps\" are present, and it's something the frog always does.","It's a general fact, so present.","Present → \"survives\"."],"Past forms make it sound like a one-time event."],"69f031ab":["F4",["Look at what comes before the blank: \"Parker's crucial finding was the first ___\".","After \"the first\", English uses \"to + verb\" (\"the first to place...\").","\"to place\""],"\"Places\" and \"placed\" would be a second main verb next to \"was.\""],"083a35dc":["B1",["Left side: \"He was instrumental in organizing the Pueblo Revolt of 1680\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"as a result of his leadership, the Spanish colonizers were expelled...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"1680, and\"."],"C is a comma splice. B has \"and\" with no comma."],"486f03da":["B2",["Left side: \"The short story 'Rogue Enchantments'... appears in Reclaim the Stars\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"an anthology of fantasy and science fiction written by authors of Latin American descent\". There's no main verb. It's a fragment renaming the book.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"Stars, an\"."],"A would leave \"An anthology…\" alone as a fragment. \"While\" adds a contrast that makes no sense."],"003f22c8":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of space shuttle mission STS-79, which lasted ten days and three hours\".","What's left: \"Every last second ___ monitored\". \"Every\" always means singular.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Days\" and \"hours\" are plural and nearby. \"Every\" always takes a singular verb."],"aab78b25":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"Psychophysicist Howard Moskowitz was hired... to determine how much artificial sweetener most people prefer in a diet drink.\"","Is it asking? No. It's describing what he was hired to figure out.","Statement → normal order (\"most people prefer\") + period."],"A and B use question order (\"do most people prefer\"). C puts a question mark on a statement."],"8b002b08":["B1",["Left side: \"This bat-and-ball game was derived from cricket\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"kilikiti differs from cricket in a few key ways\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS. The comma goes before \"but\", not after: \"cricket, but\"."],"B and D put a comma after \"but.\" A uses a colon, which doesn't fit a contrast."],"145d5ca7":["B6",["Find the core: \"challenging because of [the country's millions of miles of waterways] and [the fact that the volume... can vary]\".","Those are two noun phrases joined by \"and\", both objects of \"because of\". They aren't sentences.","No comma before \"and\" here, and none after \"that\" → \"waterways and the fact that\"."],"Commas around \"and\" or after \"that\" chop up a single \"because of X and Y\" phrase."],"843f92af":["F4",["Look at the verb before the blank: \"they rest when they need ___ their food\".","After \"need\", English uses \"to + verb\" (\"need to eat\", \"need to digest\").","\"to digest\""],"\"Need digest\" and \"need digesting\" aren't standard. \"Will digest\" is a second main verb."],"430d929a":["F2",["Who owns the findings? Watson and Crick, two people → plural.","Plural owner → \"their\". (\"They're\" = \"they are\".)","\"Their\""],"\"They're\" means \"they are.\" \"Its\" and \"It's\" are singular."],"1684b237":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"One of the few African American global explorers during the turn of the 20th century\".","Who is an explorer? Matthew Henson.","So \"Matthew Henson\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with him."],"B, C, and D start with years, Greenland, or treks, and none of those are explorers."],"be34a3df":["B6",["Find the subject: \"the Huntington Library in California\". \"In California\" is part of the subject.","Find its verb: \"received\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation."],"\"In California\" feels like an aside, but it's part of the subject."],"1f8cd95f":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"advised\" is simple past, and \"In addition\" means at the same time as the advice.","It's part of the same past moment.","Simple past → \"suggested\"."],"\"Had suggested\" would mean it happened before the advice, but \"in addition\" means it came along with it."],"73a6603c":["B1",["Left side: \"On sunny days, dark rooftops absorb solar energy and convert it to unwanted heat, raising the surrounding air temperature\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Adding a light-colored covering to an existing dark roof... helps combat this effect\". \"Adding...\" is the subject and \"helps\" is the verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"temperature. Adding\"."],"B is a comma splice. D makes \"by adding\" a modifier, but then \"helps\" has no subject."],"70ced8dc":["B1",["Left side: \"Typically, underlines, scribbles, and notes... lower a book's value\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"when the former owner is a famous poet..., such markings... can be a gold mine\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"value, but\"."],"C is a comma splice. B and D are run-ons or are missing the comma."],"3bceeb93":["F2",["Who owns the young? \"The animal\".","\"Animal\" is singular. Owned by it → \"its\" (no apostrophe).","\"its\""],"\"Platypuses\" earlier is plural, but this sentence's subject is \"the animal.\" \"It's\" = \"it is.\""],"6fac7f45":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"Butterfly... attempts to make use of the entire visual light spectrum\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains how: rainbow goggles, a rainbow swimmer, rainbow water.","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"spectrum:\"."],"A is a run-on. D's \"while\" turns an explanation into a contrast."],"aecdb820":["B1",["Left side: \"Featuring works..., the 2021 exhibition... set out to provide a wide-ranging overview... through the 1950s\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"given the collection's breadth..., its efforts were largely successful\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"1950s, and\"."],"D is a comma splice. B has \"and\" with no comma."],"2c9c6ca9":["B1",["Left side: \"In many languages, including English, these consonants are rare\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"in some dialects of Mandarin, however, four such consonants... are relatively common\". It's a full sentence (\"however\" sits inside it).","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"rare;\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"However\" is inside the second sentence, so it can't do the joining."],"8a3998f1":["B1",["Left side: \"After the United Kingdom began rolling out taxes..., plastic-bag consumption decreased by up to ninety percent\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Such taxes are subject to what economists call the 'rebound effect'\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"percent. Such\"."],"A is a comma splice. D is a run-on."],"dab8b8ee":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Fortifying soil in arid ecosystems against erosion\".","What fortifies soil? The biocrusts (these crusts).","So \"these crusts\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only C starts with it."],"A, B, and D start with an estimate, a reduction, or a study, and none of those fortify soil."],"4bed4658":["F4",["Find the pattern: \"The lock would increase the salinity of the lakes and ___ a natural barrier\".","Both verbs hang off \"would\" (\"would increase... and [would] create\").","After \"would\", use the plain form → \"create\"."],"\"Creates\" and \"created\" break the \"would ___\" pattern."],"96953201":["B6",["Find the core: \"explored themes of healing, self-discovery, and memory\". \"Of\" is a preposition and the list is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"of\"."],"A colon after \"of\" is a classic trap, because \"explored themes of\" isn't a full sentence."],"acf454af":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Using one of these noncopyrighted songs\".","An -ing action used as a subject counts as one thing, so it's singular.","Singular → \"ensures\"."],"\"Songs\" is plural and right before the blank."],"6fdddabf":["B6",["Find the core: \"The part... is called the chromophore\". The verb is \"is called\" and what it's called is \"the chromophore\".","They complete each other, and there's no aside.","Never split a verb from what completes it → \"called\"."],"It feels like a dramatic pause, but there's no grammar reason to split them."],"8f6d6ae6":["B2",["Left side: \"Archaeologists have estimated that... Cahokia... had as many as 20,000 inhabitants in the year 1150 CE\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"making it one of the largest cities in North America at the time\". It's an -ing phrase with no subject. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"CE, making\"."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"Making it…\" as a fragment."],"26c8c88c":["B2",["Left side: \"Although most meteorites are fragments of asteroids\". It starts with \"Although\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Right side: \"several hundred have been identified as being from the Moon or Mars\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"asteroids, several\"."],"A, C, and D would leave \"Although… asteroids\" as a fragment."],"c06af4d8":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Okinaka doesn't make such decisions single-handedly\" and \"all historical designations must be approved by a group...\".","\"However\" belongs with the first one: \"...single-handedly, however.\"","Semicolon after \"however\", at the real boundary: \"single-handedly, however;\"."],"B puts the semicolon before \"however,\" which wrongly attaches \"however\" to the second sentence."],"f4fd123c":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", one of over 180 annual international conferences supporting video game development,\".","What's left: \"The African Games Co-production Market ___ the growth\". One market, so singular.","Singular → \"promotes\"."],"\"Conferences\" is plural and closer to the verb."],"e8926747":["F2",["What does the blank point to? \"letters\" (eighteen of them).","\"Letters\" is plural.","Plural pointer → \"These\"."],"\"That\" and \"This\" are singular. \"One letters\" doesn't work."],"e9aee0d8":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"what had started as a local means... had grown into something far more extensive\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains what it grew into: \"a network of such committees that...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"extensive: a\"."],"C is a comma splice. B and D leave \"A network of such committees that…\" as a fragment."],"97df6650":["F1",["Find the subject: \"The village of Panduyacu in Ecuador\".","One village, so singular. \"Ecuador\" is just in a describing phrase.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Places\" later is plural, but it isn't the subject."],"d198997b":["B5",["Look at what follows the blank: \"that often incorporate words or phrases from poetry...\". It describes the scribbles.","It's essential, because it tells you which scribbles.","Essential \"that\" clause → no punctuation: \"scribbles that\"."],"Any mark before \"that\" separates the clause from the noun it describes."],"9579581e":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"divergent strategies for preserving milk emerged\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains those strategies: \"whereas rennet-coagulated... in Europe..., acid-heat coagulation methods became common among...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"emerged:\"."],"D is a comma splice. A is a run-on."],"60713427":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"among their many roles\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It ends with a comma: \"roles, provide\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma: \"that,\". Delete test: \"organic compounds that provide pigment\" ✓"],"A dash or colon wouldn't match the closing comma."],"7f1df833":["F4",["Find the subject: \"His energetic gestures... and his habit of barreling after a hit ball to see if it would land out of bounds\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb for that long subject.","Real verb → \"bounds helped\"."],"B, C, and D give the long subject no main verb at all."],"2ee50d41":["F1",["Find the subject: \"landing on one of the good spaces\".","An -ing action used as a subject counts as one thing, so it's singular.","Singular → \"allows\"."],"\"Spaces\" is plural and right before the blank."],"15d6d837":["F6",["Whose stories? Other people's. \"People\" is already plural, so add 's → \"people's\".","Do the stories own anything? No → plain plural \"stories\".","\"people's stories\""],"\"Peoples\" means several ethnic groups. \"Story's\" means one story owns something."],"59209b6d":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"since uncovering fragments of a 2,000-year-old reindeer training harness in northern Siberia\".","Who uncovered them? Researcher Robert Losey.","So \"researcher Robert Losey\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with him."],"B starts with an argument and C with domestication. Neither uncovered anything."],"a75d5984":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Several advantages\" (skip the list in dashes). Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb for \"advantages\".","Real verb → \"have positioned\"."],"\"Positioning,\" \"by positioning,\" and \"having positioned\" leave no main verb."],"2b512e65":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"ocean dwellers that include the squid, the octopus, and the cuttlefish\".","It has commas inside, so wrap it in dashes so it doesn't get confused with its own commas. Open and close with dashes.","Delete test: \"Eisenberg discovered that cephalopods have a special genetic ability\" ✓ → \"cephalopods—ocean dwellers that include...cuttlefish—\"."],"B closes the dash pair too early. C adds a colon after \"include,\" which breaks the colon rule."],"e41018de":["B1",["Find the two sentences: \"...making her one of the rare talents to earn the highest honors...—the Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, and Tony awards, respectively\" and \"the achievement is known as 'winning the EGOT'\".","The single dash opens a list that runs to the end of sentence 1, so it doesn't need a closing dash. \"Respectively\" gets a comma.","Two full sentences → semicolon after \"respectively\": \"awards, respectively;\"."],"D is a comma splice. C adds a second dash, but this aside runs to the end of the sentence."],"856b495d":["F6",["Does anything own anything? No. Several musicians introduced several audiences.","Musicians: plural, no apostrophe. Audiences: plural, no apostrophe.","\"musicians introduced audiences\""],"Apostrophes are tempting decoration. Ask what's being owned. Nothing is."],"870ae7ec":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2017, the couple converted a vacant lot in the city into an apiary\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"in the years that followed they acquired nine additional lots...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"apiary, and\"."],"A is a comma splice. C is missing the comma. D is a run-on."],"cfe23776":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"ratified in 1804\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"amendment\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"The 12th amendment separated the elections\" ✓ → \"1804,\"."],"A dash or colon wouldn't match the opening comma."],"4f2ff5f2":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"appears\" and \"symbolizes\" are present.","You describe what happens in a play in the present.","Present → \"goes\"."],"\"Went\" and \"had gone\" switch to the past. Literature is usually described in the present."],"2784cbaf":["F6",["How many variables? Five. Do they own anything? No → plain plural \"variables\".","Whose movement? Objects' in general, so many objects own it → plural possessive \"objects'\".","\"variables that characterize objects'\""],"A puts an apostrophe on \"variable's.\" D drops the possessive on \"objects.\""],"863065c7":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Legal scholars James Melton and Tom Ginsburg's analysis (of de jure judicial independence...)\".","The thing doing the identifying is \"analysis\", which is singular. The names just say whose.","Singular → \"identifies\"."],"Two names and \"decades\" pull you toward a plural verb."],"872a002e":["F2",["Who owns the peer republics? \"the Tajik Republic\" (named later in the sentence).","One republic → singular. Owned by it → \"its\".","\"its\""],"\"Their\" is plural. \"It's\" means \"it is.\""],"fcaff694":["B5",["Look at \"researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn\". The title \"researchers\" is stuck right onto the names.","Title + names → essential, so no commas. Two names joined by \"and\" → no comma either.","No punctuation → \"researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn\"."],"A and B add commas around names that are essential (you need them to know which researchers)."],"0fe5ce68":["F1",["Find the subject: \"each one of these plays\" (skip \", which include Henry V and Richard III,\").","\"Each one\" is singular. \"Of these plays\" is just a describing phrase.","Singular → \"focuses\"."],"\"Plays,\" \"Henry V,\" and \"Richard III\" make it feel plural. \"Each\" is always singular."],"b369d54c":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Woven from recycled yarn and hand tufted using a carpet weaving technique...\".","What was woven? The tapestries.","So \"the topological tapestries\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with them."],"B and D start with the artist, but she isn't woven. C starts with \"when she creates…\"."],"790fc366":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Nakalembe doesn't just compile the information\" and \"she also shares her findings with African farmers\".","\"Though\" belongs with the first one: \"...the information, though.\"","Semicolon after \"though\", at the real boundary: \"information, though;\"."],"C puts the semicolon before \"though,\" attaching it to the wrong sentence. B is a comma splice."],"62120607":["B2",["Left side: \"When viewed up close, however, the portraits reveal themselves to be quilts\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"the stitching barely visible among the thousands of pieces...\". There's no real verb (\"visible\" is a description). It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"quilts, the\"."],"A semicolon or period would leave it as a fragment. \"And\" would need a full sentence after it."],"c437dd53":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Wanting to celebrate the 100th anniversary of the Alaska Purchase\".","Who wanted to celebrate? The Alaska Centennial Commission.","So \"the Alaska Centennial Commission\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with it."],"A starts with a contest, B with an award, and C with $300. None of those can want anything."],"2bb7416a":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Like an Elvis impersonator..., an Elvis taxon is not the real thing, however\" and \"it is a misidentified look-alike\".","\"However\" belongs to the first sentence. The second starts at \"It\".","Two full sentences → period after \"however\": \"however. It\"."],"C is a comma splice disguised by \"however.\" B is a run-on."],"97b62fab":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? \"These minuscule animals\" (tardigrades).","\"Animals\" is plural.","Plural → \"they\"."],"\"It\" and \"that\" are singular. \"He\" is for a person."],"a05cc490":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Economists have observed that improvements in efficiency often correlate negatively with resource conservation\" and \"efficiency gains... may increase demand...\".","\"Though\" belongs with the first one: \"...conservation, though.\"","Semicolon after \"though\", at the real boundary: \"conservation, though;\"."],"B puts the semicolon before \"though.\" A and D create comma splices."],"89ab0d46":["B6",["Find the core: \"pages from a collection of Norse tales\". \"From\" is a preposition and \"a collection...\" is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"from\"."],"A colon looks dramatic but has no full sentence before it."],"85b8b9c5":["B1",["Left side: \"Leibniz wheel calculators were popular in the first half of the twentieth century\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"these ingenious devices were eventually replaced by electronic calculators\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"century, but\"."],"B is a comma splice. A is a run-on. \"That\" makes no sense here."],"b0a525be":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"according to Swentzell\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"figures\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"The arrangement of the figures represents her idea\" ✓ → \"Swentzell,\"."],"No punctuation leaves the aside unclosed. A colon or dash doesn't match."],"eef91a50":["B1",["Left side: \"Nine months before Rosa Parks made history..., a fifteen-year-old... Claudette Colvin was arrested for the same offense\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"According to some historians, Colvin's arrest led to Parks's action...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"offense. According\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"According to…\" looks like an add-on, but it starts a full sentence."],"01a32c84":["B1",["Left side: \"VisiCalc improved financial recordkeeping... by automatically updating all calculations that were dependent on these adjustments\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Prior to VisiCalc's release, changing a paper spreadsheet often required redoing the entire sheet...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"adjustments. Prior\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"Prior to…\" sounds like an add-on, but it opens a new sentence."],"548f4956":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"It is generally true that technological change is a linear process...\" and \"the reawakening of interest in the steam engine... reminds us...\".","\"Even so\" is a transition word, not FANBOYS, and it belongs at the start of sentence 2.","Semicolon at the boundary, then \"even so,\" → \"ones; even so,\"."],"A, B, and D are comma splices or put the punctuation on the wrong side of \"even so.\""],"9d4a701b":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"light intensity affects the chemical reaction rate of photosynthesis\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains how: \"as light intensity increases, so does the rate...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"photosynthesis:\"."],"B is a comma splice. A and D are run-ons."],"2c84f96a":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2017, artists Isabel and Ruben Toledo redesigned the costumes and sets for The Miami City Ballet's production of The Nutcracker\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"According to reviewers, the Toledos' designs helped infuse the production...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"Nutcracker. According\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"According to…\" sounds like an add-on, but a full sentence follows it."],"6181924a":["B5",["Look at \"that Palma Rodríguez uses in his art\". It tells you which materials.","It's essential, so no commas around it. Then the subject (\"The natural and mechanical materials that... art\") runs straight into its verb, \"highlight\".","No punctuation before \"that\" and none before the verb → \"materials that Palma Rodríguez uses in his art\"."],"A adds a comma between the subject and \"highlight.\" C and D put a comma before \"that.\""],"d4f173ec":["F6",["How many jugglers? Two. Do they own anything in \"both jugglers\"? No → plain plural \"jugglers\".","Whose motion? The jugglers', so plural owner → \"their\" (not \"there\" or \"they're\").","\"jugglers, regardless of their\""],"\"There\" is a place and \"they're\" = \"they are.\" \"Jugglers'\" makes the word possessive for no reason."],"dd6a0326":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Named in 1999 as one of the greatest achievements by a US chemist in the past hundred years\".","What was named an achievement? Julian's synthesis (not Julian himself).","So \"Julian's 1935 synthesis\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only C starts with it."],"A starts with \"Julian,\" but a person isn't an achievement. D starts with the chemical itself."],"4ba99a6f":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2004, Watt sewed strips of blankets together to craft a 10-by-13-inch sampler\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"in 2014, she arranged folded blankets into two large stacks...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"sampler;\"."],"C is a comma splice. A and D put \"later\" in a way that still leaves two sentences spliced."],"ce81d0b7":["B2",["Left side: \"the colorful calico rockfish... can survive for a little over a decade\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"while the rougheye rockfish... boasts a maximum life span of about two centuries\". It starts with \"while\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"decade, while\"."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"While the rougheye rockfish…\" as a fragment."],"db24ecc9":["B7",["Spot the list of three things the gardens do: growing diverse plant species, both native and nonnative / fostering scientific research / educating the public.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes, item 1 does (\"species, both native and nonnative\"). And there's a \"; and\" later.","Complex list → comma inside item 1, semicolon after it: \"species, both native and nonnative;\"."],"C puts the semicolon in the middle of the first item. A uses a comma, which blurs the items."],"35360da9":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"since it began in 2012\".","\"Since\" means from then until now.","Then until now → \"have contributed\"."],"\"Contribute\" ignores the \"since…\" span. Future forms don't fit at all."],"0fa289a7":["B1",["Left side: \"In 1955, ... Satyajit Ray released his first movie, Pather Panchali\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"A quiet black-and-white drama about a family in rural India, Ray's film was quite different...\". \"Ray's film was\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"Panchali. A\"."],"C makes a comma splice, because \"a quiet… drama\" describes the second sentence's subject, \"Ray's film.\""],"f0f9bf8f":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? Who created more installations? González-Torres.","He's one person, so singular.","Singular → \"He\"."],"\"They all,\" \"Those,\" and \"Both\" are plural, with nothing plural to refer to."],"ace95f84":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"the analysis of Earth's sediment layers (strata)\". It explains what stratigraphy is.","Find its other end. It already ends with a dash: \"(strata)—to estimate\".","Ends with a dash → starts with a dash. Delete test: \"scientists turn to stratigraphy to estimate how old a fossil is\" ✓ → \"stratigraphy—the\"."],"A colon or comma at the start wouldn't match the closing dash."],"6df020e6":["B1",["Left side: \"Before the park's construction, the surface temperature... was 0.1°C cooler than that of a control area\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"After construction, the temperature 30 meters outside of the boundary was 1.7°C cooler...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"area. After\"."],"D is a comma splice. A and B attach \"after\" to the wrong sentence."],"684b8bd2":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Far from being modern inventions\".","What could be an invention? The drinking straws.","So \"drinking straws\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with them."],"A starts with Sumerians and D with Mesopotamia. People and places aren't inventions."],"f30a478e":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"A study... offers a new explanation for the origin of Earth's continents\". It's a full sentence.","What follows names the explanation: \"geological structures called arcs, towering ridges that form...\". There's no main verb.","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"continents: geological\"."],"C and D would leave \"Geological structures called arcs, towering ridges…\" as a fragment with no verb."],"67667d72":["B1",["Left side: \"However, a team led by... Burger used accelerator mass spectrometry to uncover evidence that it was occupied earlier\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"In 1420 CE, according to Burger, humans were likely inhabiting the area\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"earlier. In\"."],"B is a comma splice. D makes it sound like it was occupied \"earlier in 1420,\" which changes the meaning."],"80aa7690":["B5",["Look at \"linguist Diane Massam\". The title \"linguist\" is stuck right onto the name.","Title + name → essential, so no commas. \"Of the University of Toronto\" attaches directly too.","No punctuation → \"linguist Diane Massam\"."],"\"A linguist, Diane Massam,\" would need \"a.\" Without it, no commas."],"cd4b1a7e":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Nery and her colleagues\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"published\"."],"\"Publishing,\" \"having published,\" and \"to publish\" aren't main verbs."],"60a4c088":["F6",["Does anything own anything? No. You place your hands between the antennas.","Two hands → plain plural. Two antennas → plain plural.","\"hands between the two antennas,\""],"Every apostrophe choice is a trap. Nothing here owns anything."],"75eb5242":["B9",["Read the clause after \"but\": \"do consumers actually prefer this simpler style?\"","Is it really asking? Yes. The next sentence says the researchers set out to find out.","Real question → question order (\"do consumers... prefer\") + question mark."],"A and D use statement order. C has question order with a period."],"dd428136":["F6",["Whose physical properties? The snow grains', and there are many grains → plural possessive \"grains'\".","Do the properties own anything? No → plain plural \"properties\".","\"grains' physical properties\""],"D drops the apostrophe. C makes \"property's\" possessive for no reason."],"a94b7fd6":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"back in the 1860s\".","That's the past.","Past → \"was\"."],"\"Is\" and \"will be\" ignore the 1860s."],"c7cb5186":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", not its population size,\".","What's left: \"a state's voting record and polling data ___\". X and Y → plural.","Plural → \"determine\"."],"\"Population size\" is singular and right before the verb, but it's inside the aside."],"6bc907c9":["F6",["Whose novels? Austen's, one person → \"Austen's\".","Do the novels own anything? No → plain plural \"novels\". Then a comma before the title that renames one of them.","\"Austen's most famous novels,\""],"\"Austens'\" means several Austens. \"Novel's\" makes the novel own something."],"04bfd364":["B1",["Left side: \"The chemical trimethylamine N-oxide (TMAO) counters this effect, ensuring that proteins retain their original configurations\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"TMAO is found in high concentrations in the cells of the deepest-dwelling fish\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"configurations. TMAO\"."],"C is a comma splice. B is a run-on."],"1e274153":["F4",["Find the subject: \"more than 10,000 women\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"served\"."],"\"Having served,\" \"to serve,\" and \"serving\" leave the sentence without a main verb."],"36868920":["B1",["Left side: \"What increases is the rate at which the water turns to vapor\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"With a pressure cooker pot, though, an airtight seal traps the vapor in the pot...\". \"Seal traps\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"vapor. With\"."],"C is a comma splice. \"With…\" sounds like an add-on, but a full sentence follows it."],"772835db":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Defined by a disorganized molecular structure\".","Which ice has a disorganized structure? Amorphous ice (ice Ih is the crystal one).","So \"amorphous ice\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with it."],"A and D start with ice Ih (the crystal one). C starts with \"the lack of thermal energy.\""],"f339ee39":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Multiple newspapers\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"serve\"."],"\"Having served,\" \"to serve,\" and \"serving\" leave no main verb."],"ea8f4658":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"which is known as Brownian motion\". You could delete it.","Find its other end. It ends with a comma: \"Brownian motion, can be observed\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma. Delete test: \"This type of random, continuous movement can be observed\" ✓ → \"movement, which\"."],"C has no opening comma. A and D break the sentence apart."],"f768090a":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read past the blank: \"These extensive changes... reflected the poet's evolving literary perspective\". \"Reflected\" is the main verb.","So the blank begins an aside listing the changes, which ends at the comma after \"material\".","Aside → comma + -ing: \"changes, including\"."],"\"Included\" or \"would include\" would give the subject two main verbs."],"775f3eb9":["F6",["Does anything own anything? No. He tells stories about immigrants.","Many stories → plain plural. Many immigrants → plain plural.","\"stories of the South Asian immigrants\""],"\"Story's\" and \"immigrant's\" add apostrophes where nothing owns anything."],"5670a657":["B1",["Sentence 1 runs: \"certain events were pivotal: the founding of... LULAC in 1929, for one\". \"For one\" belongs to it, because it's the first example.","Sentence 2: \"The Katzenbach v. Morgan court decision in 1966... is another such event\". It's a full sentence.","Comma before \"for one\", period after it: \"1929, for one. The\"."],"A attaches \"for one\" to the second sentence. B is a comma splice."],"b6560e5a":["B1",["Left side: \"...Charpagne and her colleagues believed they could improve on... NiCoCr... by replacing chromium with ruthenium (Ru)\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"the alloy that resulted, NiCoRu, turned out to be an unsuitable replacement\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"(Ru), but\"."],"C is a comma splice. B has \"but\" with no comma."],"5aa171de":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"They are hardly pristine\" and \"many chondrites experience aqueous alteration...\".","\"Though\" belongs with the first one: \"They are hardly pristine, though.\"","Semicolon after \"though\", at the real boundary: \"pristine, though;\"."],"C puts the semicolon before \"though.\" A and D are comma splices."],"5b8f9cf2":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"A coming-of-age story, a social meditation, and a sober gaze into the dark maw of French colonialism\".","What is a coming-of-age story? The novel (not Chraïbi, and not Morocco).","So \"Chraïbi's debut novel\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with it."],"A starts with Morocco and C with Chraïbi the author. A person isn't a \"coming-of-age story.\""],"b5b74c3f":["F1",["Find the subject: \"her writing\".","\"Writing\" is singular, and \"before\" points to the present perfect.","Singular → \"has been\"."],"\"Were,\" \"have been,\" and \"are\" are plural."],"432b1ede":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"in this case, the porous rocks of the hills around Hot Springs\".","Find its other end. It starts with a dash after \"earth—\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. Delete test: \"rainwater percolates downward through the earth to collect in a subterranean basin\" ✓ → \"Springs—to\"."],"A comma or colon at the end wouldn't match the opening dash."],"c21df211":["B2",["Left side: \"Theaters were fitted with specialized vents that emitted odors at specific points in a movie\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"such as the scent of roses when roses appeared in a scene\". \"Such as...\" has no main verb. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"movie, such\"."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"Such as…\" as a fragment."],"50445680":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"those food sources may become unavailable because of extensive snow and ice cover\". It's already complete.","The blank adds a result, with no new subject.","Result add-on → comma + -ing: \"forcing\"."],"\"Forces\" or \"forced\" would be a second main verb with no subject or \"and.\""],"267a13e2":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2010, archaeologist Noel Hidalgo Tan was visiting... when he noticed markings of red paint on the temple walls\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"With the help of digital imaging techniques, he discovered the markings to be part of an elaborate mural...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"walls. With\"."],"A is a comma splice. C's \"so with\" is ungrammatical."],"403d7bb5":["B6",["Find the subject: \"the reason seeds from a dying dandelion appear to float in the air while falling\". \"While falling\" is part of it.","Find its verb: \"is\" (right after the blank).","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation after \"falling\"."],"A comma feels natural before \"is,\" but it would split the subject from its verb."],"fcab3630":["B1",["Left side: \"For centuries, scholars dreamed about locating a copy of this legendary lost work\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"That fantasy (partially) became reality in 2022...\". \"That fantasy\" is a subject and \"became\" is its verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"work. That\"."],"B and D read \"that\" as a connector, but \"that fantasy\" is a new subject."],"a7fdf862":["B7",["Spot the list of places: libraries / train stations / public squares.","Do the items have commas inside? No, it's a simple list.","Simple list → commas: \"libraries, train\"."],"A colon or dash after \"such as\" breaks the list. \"Such as\" never takes a colon."],"21e58a83":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Jetties can sometimes have the opposite effect\" and \"obstructing the natural flow of sand... can lead to increased erosion...\".","\"Though\" belongs with the first one: \"...the opposite effect, though.\"","Semicolon after \"though\", at the real boundary: \"effect, though;\"."],"C puts the semicolon before \"though.\" B and D are comma splices."],"de3dd17d":["F4",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"bits of molten rock... that, after cooling and solidifying into glass, ___ back onto Mars's surface\".","A \"that\" clause needs a real verb. Its subject, \"bits\", is plural.","Real, plural verb → \"rain\"."],"\"To rain,\" \"raining,\" and \"having rained\" aren't real verbs, so the \"that\" clause would have no verb."],"c102f9b0":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? The whole event: Minh becoming secretary-general.","One event → singular.","Singular → \"this\"."],"\"These,\" \"those,\" and \"some\" are plural."],"6b49f5f1":["B1",["Left side: \"Many, including poet Alexander Pope, were skeptical\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"historians have determined that Shakespeare's company did perform a play called Cardenio in 1613\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"skeptical, but\"."],"C is a comma splice. A has \"but\" with no comma."],"ecba68b5":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", sometimes in conjunction with mathematicians Richard S. Hamilton and Shing-Tung Yau,\".","What's left: \"Mathematician Grigori Perelman ___ credited\". One person, so singular.","Singular → \"is\"."],"The aside names two more people, which makes it feel plural, but asides don't change the subject."],"2dd1b8bf":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Compared to that of alumina glass\". \"That of\" means \"the atomic arrangement of\".","So you're comparing arrangements. The thing after the comma has to be an arrangement.","\"silica glass's atomic arrangement\" is the only choice that starts with an arrangement."],"A and B start with \"silica glass\" itself. C starts with \"a disadvantage.\""],"577b09fa":["B7",["Spot the list of three things mosses can do: clone themselves / enter a dormant state in times of drought / grow in areas that don't have soil.","Do the items have commas inside? No, it's a simple list.","Simple list → commas: \"themselves,\"."],"A semicolon is only for lists whose items have commas inside. A period breaks the list apart."],"59094d87":["B1",["Left side: \"The Tantaquidgeon Museum... was founded in 1931 with the goal of showcasing the culture and history of the Mohegan Tribe\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"today, nearly a century later, it is the oldest Native-owned... museum\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"Tribe, and\"."],"D is a comma splice. C has \"and\" with no comma."],"235c8338":["B1",["Left side: \"That the expansionist appetites... had their sights set on his country was evident to Siam's King Chulalongkorn\". The whole \"That...\" clause is the subject and \"was evident\" is the verb, so it's a full sentence.","Right side: \"deterring those ambitions through... diplomacy and domestic reform was perhaps his foremost achievement\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"Chulalongkorn;\"."],"C is a comma splice. The first sentence starting with \"That\" makes it hard to spot that it's complete."],"b771d175":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"Compiling isn't exclusive to certain programming languages\" and \"any language can incorporate this tool\".","\"However\" belongs with the first one: \"...certain programming languages, however.\"","Semicolon after \"however\", at the real boundary: \"languages, however;\"."],"B puts the semicolon before \"however,\" attaching it to the second sentence, which gives the wrong meaning."],"8772475b":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"When it comes to their diets, goats are notoriously indiscriminate\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains what that means: \"they will devour all kinds of shrubs and weeds...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"indiscriminate:\"."],"B is a comma splice. C is a run-on."],"eee7536f":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"On March 27, 2004\" and \"retired\" (past).","Her career ended then.","Past → \"finished\"."],"\"Has been finishing\" and \"will be finishing\" ignore that her career ended in 2004."],"0ff8477b":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"connects\" and \"prepares\" are present.","You describe novels in the present.","Present → \"experiences\"."],"\"Experienced\" or \"had experienced\" switch to the past for no reason."],"b260c65a":["F6",["How many Moons? One. Does it own something? Yes, the gravitational pull.","One owner → 's.","\"Moon's\""],"\"Moons\" is plural with no ownership. \"Moons'\" means several moons own the pull."],"83c9c503":["B6",["Find the subject: \"Her portrait of novelist Zadie Smith\".","Find its verb: \"is displayed\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation: \"Smith\"."],"The long subject tempts a pause. Don't."],"05a14e18":["B7",["Spot the list of three elements: intricate patterns of interlocking spiral lines, which often held symbolic significance / depictions of birds, horses, and other animals / inlaid enamel accents.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes, several do. And there's a \"; and\" later.","Complex list → semicolon between items: \"significance; depictions\"."],"B uses a comma, which blurs where the first item ends."],"9f737b2a":["F6",["Whose sediment? One playa (the Racetrack Playa) → \"playa's\".","Whose migration? The rocks', and there are many rocks → plural possessive \"rocks'\".","\"playa's sediment mark the rocks'\""],"B forgets that the migration belongs to the rocks. D makes \"playas\" plural, but there's only one playa."],"c52652c9":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What finds faces in clouds? \"The human brain\".","\"Brain\" is singular and it's a thing, not a person.","Singular thing → \"it\"."],"\"She\" is for a person. \"They\" and \"those\" are plural."],"ba8ebf49":["B1",["Left side: \"The poem Beowulf begins with the word 'hwæt,' which is an Old English exclamation\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Translated as 'hark!' or 'listen!' in some versions, the word was playfully rendered as 'bro!'...\". \"The word was rendered\" is a subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"exclamation. Translated\"."],"A is a comma splice. \"Translated…\" belongs to the second sentence's subject, \"the word.\""],"188f7e3c":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"By reducing the runoff flowing into city sewers\".","What reduced the runoff? The bioswales.","So \"the bioswales\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with them."],"A and C start with \"the mitigation.\" D starts with \"street flooding,\" which certainly didn't reduce runoff."],"36944347":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What accumulates sediment? \"rivers\" (\"rivers such as the Mississippi\").","\"Rivers\" is plural.","Plural → \"they accumulate\"."],"\"The Mississippi\" is singular, but it's an example inside \"rivers such as the Mississippi.\""],"a466679a":["B6",["Find the core: \"guitar riffs effortlessly blend with... vocal melodies\". The verb \"blend\" is finished by \"with...\".","There's no aside or boundary here.","Never split a verb from the phrase that finishes it → no punctuation: \"blend with\"."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"With Charlie Adams's… melodies\" as a fragment."],"a8fa749a":["B7",["Spot the list of three works: The Joys of Motherhood, a novel... / A Kind of Marriage, a television play... / Head Above Water, her autobiography.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes, each title has a comma before its description. And there's a \"; and\" later.","Semicolon between items, comma between title and description → \"Lagos; A Kind of Marriage,\"."],"A uses a comma, which blurs the items. D drops the comma between the title and its description."],"898f182c":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"Between 1907 and 1946, he patented many inventions\". It's already complete.","The blank introduces examples, with no new subject.","Add-on → comma + -ing: \"including\"."],"\"Included\" or \"includes\" would be a second main verb with no subject."],"6e5bf3a8":["F4",["Find the subject: \"A recent study\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"explains\"."],"\"Explaining,\" \"having explained,\" and \"to explain\" leave no main verb."],"fa36d803":["B5",["Look at \"little-known French portrait artist Marie-Denise Villers\". The descriptor runs right into the name.","Title + name → essential, so no punctuation (there's no \"a\" or \"the\" before it).","No punctuation → \"artist\"."],"There's no \"a\" or \"the\" before the title, so no comma."],"84658166":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"Hopper's subsequent career would involve more than just equations, though\". It's a full sentence (\"though\" belongs to it).","What follows explains it: \"as a pioneering computer programmer, Hopper would help usher in the digital age\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon after \"though\": \"equations, though:\"."],"B is a comma splice. C attaches \"Though\" to the wrong sentence."],"7c48a6dd":["F4",["Find the pattern: \"He applied bold, saturated hues to large canvases and ___ them from ceilings or walls\".","The two verbs share \"He\" and are joined by \"and\", so they must match \"applied\".","Match → \"suspended\"."],"\"Suspending\" and \"to suspend\" break the \"applied… and suspended\" pattern."],"e3c1b4f7":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"though\". It's an interrupter you could delete.","Find its other end. It ends with a comma: \"though, she occasionally relies\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma: \"work, though,\". The intro \"To manifest... of her work\" isn't a full sentence, so no semicolon."],"C and D put a semicolon in, but \"To manifest the… atmospheres… of her work\" isn't a full sentence."],"5cc85f01":["B2",["Left side: \"A conceptual artist and designer embraced by both the art world and the fashion world\". There's no main verb. It's a fragment describing Mary Ping.","Right side: \"Mary Ping was chosen to curate the exhibition...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"world,\"."],"A semicolon or colon would treat the intro phrase like a full sentence."],"8d53e7a0":["F6",["Do the poems own anything? No → plain plural \"poems\".","Whose protagonist? The novel's, and there's one novel → \"novel's\".","\"poems putatively written by the novel's\""],"\"Poem's\" and \"novels'\" put the apostrophes in the wrong places."],"43b88827":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"which brought an end to the Thirty Years' War\".","What's left: \"The treaties ___ signed\". \"Treaties\" is plural.","Plural → \"were\"."],"\"War\" is singular and sits right before the verb."],"cabe71d4":["B5",["Look at what follows the blank: \"that specializes in the creation and promotion of stories...\". It defines the company.","It's essential, because it tells you what kind of company. Without \"that\", \"specializes\" would have no subject.","Essential \"that\" clause → no comma: \"company that\"."],"A and C leave \"specializes\" with no subject. D puts a comma before \"that.\""],"9994ae0d":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", utilizing elements as disparate as plant-dyed fabrics, wire crab traps filled with stones, and cotton balls dangling above a fan,\".","What's left: \"Barrada's pieces ___ the ways\". \"Pieces\" is plural.","Plural → \"explore\"."],"\"A fan\" and \"cotton balls\" sit near the verb, and \"Barrada\" is singular."],"99dedf36":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? Go back to \"a given term\".","\"Term\" is singular. The examples in dashes are just an aside.","Singular → \"it often becomes\"."],"The examples in dashes (\"self-fulfilling prophecies\" and \"role models\") are plural, but they're inside an aside."],"61160f0a":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read past the blank: \"Author Madeline L'Engle... begins her novel\". \"Begins\" is the main verb.","So the blank begins an aside about L'Engle that ends at the comma after \"in\".","Aside → -ing form: \"looking\"."],"\"Looked,\" \"looks,\" and \"is looking\" would give \"L'Engle\" two main verbs."],"7b950fc2":["B1",["Left side: \"In 2000, Nora de Hoyos Comstock... sought to increase Latina representation in corporate settings\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"She founded Las Comadres para las Americas...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"settings. She\"."],"B is a comma splice. A is a run-on."],"75f49353":["F1",["Flip the sentence: \"Josephine St. Pierre Ruffin ___ among the clubs' leaders\".","The subject is Ruffin, one person. \"Leaders\" is just in the \"among...\" phrase.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Leaders\" comes before the verb and looks like the subject, but it's inside \"among the clubs' leaders.\""],"20a6a4ed":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", of the Universidade Federal Fluminense in Brazil,\".","What's left: \"Anthropologist Lívia Barbosa ___ food and sociability\". One person, so singular.","Singular → \"studies\"."],"The long university name pushes the verb away from the subject."],"2cb1ee22":["F2",["Whose shores? \"the North Sea\".","One sea → singular. Owned by it → \"its\" (no apostrophe).","\"its\""],"\"Peoples, cultures, and economies\" are plural, but they're not the owner. \"It's\" = \"it is.\""],"a03008de":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", common ancestor of fifty-five African languages with similar linguistic properties,\".","What's left: \"The Proto-Nilotic language ___ hypothetical\". One language, so singular. The blank also opens the aside \"like all protolanguages\", so it needs a comma.","Singular + comma → \"is,\"."],"\"Languages\" and \"protolanguages\" are plural and sit near the verb."],"c88ba1b7":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"this period in automotive design is remembered for its grandeur and artistry\". It's complete.","After the comma, \"its vehicles ___ by collectors\" is a describing add-on, not a second sentence.","Describing add-on → -ed form, no helping verb: \"highly prized\"."],"\"Are\" or \"were\" + \"prized\" would turn the add-on into a second sentence and create a comma splice."],"e7afd0a1":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"The radial velocity method... has detected previously unknown exoplanets at vast distances from Earth\". It's a full sentence.","What follows is a list of planets, and each item is \"at X light-years away, [planet]\" (look at item 2).","Full sentence + list → colon, then match item 2's pattern: \"Earth: at 41 light-years away,\"."],"D puts no colon before the list. C uses a comma, which can't introduce a list."],"1b97cce9":["B1",["Left side: \"Archaeologist Laila Nehmé recently traveled to Hegra to study its ancient tombs\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Built into the rocky outcrops of a vast desert, these burial chambers seem to blend...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"tombs. Built\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"Built…\" describes the second sentence's subject, \"these burial chambers.\""],"952fd392":["B1",["Left side: \"Alberto Gabriele... tracks the transnational dissemination of works by... Braddon via the magazine Belgravia\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Published from 1866 to 1899 and distributed throughout..., the magazine... was...\" It has its own subject + verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"Belgravia. Published\"."],"D is a comma splice. A semicolon would also work, but A puts it where it leaves a fragment."],"fff4c7f4":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? Her \"poems\" / \"these works\".","They're plural.","Plural → \"them\"."],"\"This,\" \"that,\" and \"it\" are singular."],"40c3589d":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"a combination of fiction and memoir, poetry and prose\".","Find its other end. It starts with a dash: \"The Women Are Singing—a combination\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. Delete test: \"Her book Sáanii Dahataal... serves as a testament\" ✓ → \"prose—\"."],"A comma would open with a dash and close with a comma, which doesn't match."],"b15724fc":["B1",["Left side: \"American writer Edwidge Danticat... has won acclaim for her powerful short stories, novels, and essays\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Praising her lyrical yet unflinching depictions..., writer Robert Antoni has compared Danticat to... Morrison\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"essays. Praising\"."],"A is a comma splice. \"Praising…\" describes the second sentence's subject, Robert Antoni."],"c5d39bc7":["F2",["Who attaches to the shell? The barnacles attach themselves (the action goes back onto the subject).","\"Barnacles\" is plural.","Plural reflexive → \"themselves\"."],"\"Itself\" and \"it\" are singular. \"Them\" would mean some other things."],"e2759b92":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"from 1440 to 1473\".","That's long past.","Past → \"was\"."],"\"Has been\" and \"is\" make it sound ongoing."],"72b3db98":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"which takes place in a mid-twentieth-century rural Alaskan fishing village\".","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after the title \"Under Nushagak Bluff\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"Mia Heavener's 2019 novel... tells the story\" ✓ → \"village, tells\"."],"No comma would leave the aside unclosed. A period would cut off the verb \"tells.\""],"a872c60a":["B1",["Left side: \"The pedagogy of the Suzuki method is rooted in several central tenets\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Developed by Japanese violinist Shinichi Suzuki..., the method emphasizes playing instruments...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"tenets. Developed\"."],"D is a comma splice. \"Developed…\" describes the second sentence's subject, \"the method.\""],"615f001e":["B1",["Left side: \"A ray diagram reveals how this works\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Because the hole's small size restricts light to a single ray, all light passing through the hole can only arrive at a single destination\". It's a full sentence (it opens with a \"because\" intro).","Two full sentences → period: \"works. Because\"."],"A blends the \"because\" clause into the first sentence, which leaves the rest dangling. C is a comma splice."],"594b4a94":["B5",["Look at \"American geologist Marie Tharp\". The title is stuck right onto the name.","Title + name → essential. You need her name to finish \"owes much to...\".","No punctuation → \"geologist\"."],"A, C, and D cut the name off from what it completes."],"5ad2a12c":["F4",["Find the subject: \"The collection\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"includes\"."],"\"Including,\" \"to include,\" and \"to be including\" leave no main verb."],"02871a0d":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"the sculpture's steel panels have a crisscrossing pattern inspired by the iron gates Soto would see in her neighborhood.\"","Is it asking? No. It's telling you where the pattern came from.","Statement → normal order (\"Soto would see\") + period."],"C and D flip into question order (\"would Soto see\"). A adds a question mark to a statement."],"2fd05c15":["B6",["Find the subject: \"her 2019 novel Gingerbread\".","Find its verb: \"offers\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation: \"Gingerbread\"."],"A comma after the title feels natural, but it would split the subject from the verb."],"1448f43f":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? \"Multitask learning\" (the approach just named).","One approach → singular.","Singular → \"It\"."],"\"Those,\" \"they,\" and \"some\" are plural."],"175df826":["F4",["The blank is inside a \"how\" clause: \"how his barbershop... ___ as a political hub\".","That clause needs a real verb for \"barbershop\".","Real verb → \"served\"."],"\"Serving,\" \"having served,\" and \"to serve\" aren't real verbs."],"bce9910f":["F1",["Find the subject: \"The sculpture\".","One sculpture → singular.","Singular → \"stands\"."],"\"Are standing,\" \"have been standing,\" and \"were standing\" are all plural."],"c88d6301":["B7",["Spot the list of three films: Zoot Suit (1981) / El Norte (1983) / Stand and Deliver (1988).","Do the items have commas inside? No. The dates are just tags in parentheses.","Simple list → commas after each date: \"(1981), El Norte (1983),\"."],"D drops the commas. B's dashes treat El Norte like an aside."],"aacddbd8":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"acquiring a sophisticated timbre and precise control over pitch, dynamics, and vibrato\".","Find its other end. It starts with a dash after \"abilities—\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. Delete test: \"As jazz singer Sarah Vaughan honed her vocal abilities she earned comparisons\" ✓ → \"vibrato—\"."],"A comma would be swallowed up by the list's commas and wouldn't match the opening dash."],"97e7bedc":["F1",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"nematodes that ___ in contact with it\".","\"That\" refers to \"nematodes\", which is plural.","Plural → \"come\"."],"\"Toxin\" is singular and nearby, but the nematodes are the ones coming into contact."],"c8540a5b":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"called ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay)\".","Find its other end. It ends with a comma after the parenthesis: \"assay), is used\".","Ends with a comma → starts with a comma. Delete test: \"Their method is used to detect\" ✓ → \"method, called\"."],"A and B open with a parenthesis or dash, but the aside closes with a comma."],"e15c50b2":["B7",["Spot the list of three things advanced floats can do: measure a wider range of variables / travel to greater depths / monitor seasonal ice zones.","Do the items have commas inside? No, it's a simple list.","Simple list → commas: \"variables, travel to greater depths,\"."],"A's colon has no full sentence introducing a list. D's semicolon is only for complex lists."],"988c78eb":["F1",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"a document that ___ the basic freedoms\".","\"That\" refers to \"document\", which is singular.","Singular → \"outlines\"."],"\"Roosevelt and Mehta\" are two people, but they aren't the subject of the \"that\" clause."],"261f4ca6":["B6",["Find the core: \"the Aztec people created floating farms\". \"Created\" is the verb and \"floating farms\" is its object.","Never split a verb from its object.","No punctuation → \"created\"."],"A colon might seem to introduce \"floating farms,\" but colons need a full sentence before them."],"96499989":["B6",["Find the core: \"Fans... will appreciate the multiple references\". \"Appreciate\" is the verb and \"the multiple references\" is its object.","Never split a verb from its object.","No punctuation → \"appreciate the\"."],"A colon or period would cut \"appreciate\" off from what's being appreciated."],"fdb16e20":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"in the process\". You could delete it (\"alter the particles and dismantle any information\").","It needs a matching pair around it.","Two commas → \"and, in the process,\"."],"A and D have only one comma. C mixes a comma and a dash."],"7e37affc":["B1",["Left side: \"That the geographic center of North America lay in... North Dakota was conceded by all involved\". The \"That...\" clause is the subject and \"was conceded\" is the verb, so it's a full sentence.","Right side: \"establishing its precise coordinates proved more divisive\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"involved;\"."],"B is a comma splice. A colon would mean the second part explains the first, but it's a contrast."],"28166dc6":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", each with a crossing number below five,\".","What's left: \"The trefoil knot and the figure-eight knot ___\". X and Y → plural.","Plural → \"are\"."],"\", each with a crossing number below five,\" uses singular \"each\" to distract you."],"4565a53c":["B9",["Read the clause after the comma: \"could the blueberries thrive?\"","Is it really asking? Yes. The next sentence says Michel was determined to find out.","Real question → question order (\"could the blueberries\") + question mark."],"B and C use statement order. A has question order with a period."],"f40b447c":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"Zhang Daqian's 1983 painting... is inspired by the tradition of qinglü shanshui\". It's complete.","\"a type of Chinese landscape painting ___ by the use of blue and green hues\" describes that tradition. It's not a new sentence.","Describing phrase → -ed form, no helping verb: \"characterized\"."],"\"Is characterized\" would turn the description into a second main verb."],"7a0d9031":["F1",["Find the subject: \"the triangle representing the mountain itself\".","\"Triangle\" is singular. \"Representing the mountain\" just describes it.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Figures\" and \"paintings\" later are plural, and the subject is separated from the verb."],"91fbd59d":["B2",["Left side: \"While filming a boxing match for the movie Body and Soul (1947)\". It starts with \"While\" and has no subject. It's a fragment.","Right side: \"Howe had a handheld camera operator wear roller skates\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"(1947),\"."],"A and C add \"and,\" which treats the intro like a full sentence."],"a421f749":["B1",["Left side: \"the Puerto Rican municipality of Aibonito is well known for its lush greenery\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"this distinction has earned it the fitting nickname of 'the Garden of Puerto Rico'\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → semicolon: \"greenery;\"."],"B is a comma splice. C has \"and\" with no comma."],"d46ac7e7":["F3",["Find the timeline: he joined the Knicks in 1947 (past). The war (1941–45) came before that.","A past event that happened before another past event uses \"had\".","Earlier past → \"had already served\"."],"\"Already served\" and \"was already serving\" don't show that the war came first."],"b1e8b87f":["B6",["Find the core: \"archeologists have unearthed knives, horseshoes, wool tunics, and thousands of other artifacts\". The list is the object of \"unearthed\".","Never split a verb from its object, even a long list.","No punctuation → \"unearthed\"."],"A colon looks like it introduces the list, but \"archeologists have unearthed\" isn't a full sentence without its object."],"2b6e1c06":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"A government body officially known as the Althing\".","What is a government body? Iceland's parliament.","So \"Iceland's parliament\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with it."],"A starts with \"the world's oldest parliaments,\" C with \"the first meeting,\" and D with \"930 CE.\""],"3595a991":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What was exhibited? The \"two memorable woodcuts\".","\"Woodcuts\" is plural.","Plural → \"they were\"."],"\"It\" and \"this\" are singular. \"Some\" suggests only part of them."],"f45ae404":["B1",["Left side: \"The city of Amsterdam partnered with consultants to develop Public Eye—an ethical AI-powered crowd-monitoring system\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Analyzing video streamed from cameras..., the AI algorithm determines crowd sizes...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"system. Analyzing\"."],"B is a comma splice. \"Analyzing…\" describes the second sentence's subject, the algorithm."],"1ee4485c":["B4",["Is there a second full sentence? Look after the blank: \"feeling that it didn't encapsulate...\" There's no subject, so it's a fragment. Only one sentence here.","So \"however\" is just an interrupter in the middle: \"Scott-Heron himself resisted the godfather nickname, however, feeling that...\".","Interrupter → matching pair of commas: \"nickname, however,\"."],"B and D add a semicolon, which would leave \"feeling that it didn't…\" as a fragment."],"e31b0056":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Mary Madden of Ohio\".","One person → singular.","Singular → \"was\"."],"\"Are,\" \"have been,\" and \"were\" are plural."],"8459dc2f":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"Click restraint is the practice of scanning a search results page... before deciding which link you should choose.\"","Is it asking? No. It's defining click restraint.","Statement → normal order (\"you should choose\") + period."],"A and C flip into question order. B adds a question mark to a statement."],"31362d2d":["F3",["Read the timeline: \"If sugars ___ Earth... the sugars could have reacted with other molecules\". It's a \"what if\" about the distant past.","A past \"what if\" uses \"had\" in the if-part.","\"had reached\""],"\"Reach\" and \"will reach\" talk about the present or future."],"667a0587":["B2",["Left side: \"This changed in 1735 with the passage of the Engravers' Copyright Act\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"which gave engravers control over the distribution...\". It starts with \"which\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"Act, which\"."],"A semicolon or period would leave \"Which gave…\" as a fragment."],"d2cf0e11":["F6",["How many screws? One. Does it own the threads? Yes → \"screw's\".","Do the threads own anything? No → plain plural \"threads\".","\"screw's threads.\""],"\"Screws'\" means several screws. \"Thread's\" adds an apostrophe where nothing is owned."],"1d971f75":["F6",["How many photographers? One (Adams). Does he own the photos? Yes → \"photographer's\".","Do the photos own anything? No → plain plural \"photos\".","\"photographer's early photos\""],"\"Photo's\" with an apostrophe is a classic mistake for a simple plural."],"2bca654a":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Forming extensive networks via mycorrhizal association\".","Who forms networks? The pine trees and the fungus (living things, not \"nutrients\" or \"transport\").","So \"pine trees and the fungus...\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with them."],"A, B, and C start with \"it,\" \"the transport,\" or \"nutrients,\" and none of those form networks."],"f40ca576":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"each year\" and \"journey\" (present).","It's a yearly event, a general fact.","Present → \"fly\"."],"\"Flew\" and \"had flown\" make it sound like a one-time past event."],"c101fc44":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"in 2017 a team... tried something new\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains the new thing: \"sequencing the DNA found in Neanderthals' fossilized dental plaque\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"new: sequencing\"."],"B and D would leave \"Sequencing the DNA…\" as a fragment."],"0bcb4417":["F2",["Whose eight panels? Read on: \"the poems present viewers...\". The panels belong to the poems.","\"Poems\" is plural. Plural owner → \"their\".","\"their\""],"\"Its\" is singular. \"It's\" and \"they're\" are contractions."],"dddfa043":["B2",["Left side: \"By linking the Hudson River to Lake Erie\". There's no subject or main verb. It's a fragment (an intro phrase).","Right side: \"the canal reduced transport time to nine days...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"Erie, the\"."],"A semicolon or colon would treat the intro phrase like a full sentence."],"be37d4ae":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"They were not the first in England to adopt the literary modes of classical antiquity\" and \"some of the most prominent figures... were also influenced...\".","\"However\" belongs with the first one: \"...classical antiquity, however.\"","Semicolon after \"however\", at the real boundary: \"antiquity, however;\"."],"D puts the semicolon before \"however.\" A and B are comma splices."],"94de423a":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"Like most star parties, Okie-Tex takes place in an area with low light pollution\". It's complete.","The blank adds a result, with no new subject.","Result add-on → -ing: \"ensuring\"."],"\"It ensures\" or \"this will ensure\" after a comma would make a comma splice."],"c8607bdf":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Alexander's use of 'we'\".","The thing doing the evoking is \"use\", which is singular. \"We\" is just in the \"of\" phrase.","Singular → \"evokes\"."],"\"We\" is plural and sits right before the verb, which makes it a nasty trap."],"b6de636f":["B1",["Left side: \"At 1,280 meters, the Golden Gate Bridge... is one of the longest suspension bridges in the world\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"the Tsing Ma Bridge in China, at 1,377 meters, is even longer\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"world, but\"."],"C is a comma splice. B puts an extra comma after \"but.\""],"c15069eb":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"Only five of these dances are included in international ballroom dance competitions, however\". It's a full sentence.","What follows is a list of the five dances: rumba, samba, cha-cha-cha, paso doble, and jive.","Full sentence + list → colon after \"however\": \"competitions, however:\"."],"C and D use a semicolon, but what follows is a list, not a sentence."],"5aae2475":["B5",["Look at \"plant cell biologist Yuree Lee\". The title is stuck right onto the name.","Title + name → essential, so no commas. Then the subject runs straight into its verb, \"showed\".","No punctuation → \"biologist Yuree Lee\"."],"B adds a comma between the subject and \"showed.\" A and D add commas around the name."],"db2e480a":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"had drastically increased\" is past.","\"By the time he ___ the throne\" is the past moment we're measuring from.","Simple past → \"ascended\"."],"Present and future forms clash with \"had increased.\""],"4c9a2aee":["F1",["Find the subject: \"Sound-based tools\".","\"Tools\" is plural.","Plural → \"enable\"."],"\"Has enabled,\" \"is enabling,\" and \"enables\" are singular."],"b0fb36ad":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of what cross-cultural traits distinguish these distinct modes, and secondarily what pressures led humans to develop them in the first place\".","What's left: \"The question ___ neuropsychologist Daniela Sammler's 2024 study\". \"Question\" is singular.","Singular → \"animates\"."],"\"Modes,\" \"pressures,\" and \"humans\" are all plural and sit closer to the blank."],"a30567fd":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"Her broad singing repertoire... lent Fairuz a timeless, cross-generational appeal\". It's complete.","The blank adds a result, with no new subject.","Result add-on → -ing: \"earning\"."],"\"Earned\" would be a second main verb with no \"and.\" That's a comma splice in disguise."],"12013e06":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"assuming solar panel installers' cooperation\".","Find its other end. It starts with a dash: \"indicate that—assuming\".","Starts with a dash → ends with a dash. Delete test: \"Walston's findings indicate that this practice has the potential to increase...\" ✓ → \"cooperation—this\"."],"A comma or colon wouldn't match the opening dash."],"f1c5157d":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Established in 1936 by African American novelist Richard Wright\".","What was established? The South Side Writers Group.","So \"the South Side Writers Group\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with it."],"B and C start with writers and D with Chicago. Those weren't \"established.\""],"96a904c5":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Laden with emojis and seemingly meaningless words, and driven largely by Sawano's 'personal feeling and mood'\".","What is full of emojis? The titles.","So \"the titles\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with them."],"A starts with the listener and C with each piece. Neither is laden with emojis."],"5cc69ee1":["B4",["Is there a second full sentence? Look after the blank: \"recommending evolving binary star systems...\" There's no subject, so it's a fragment. Only one sentence here.","So \"however\" is just an interrupter: \"Lai and Muñoz recognize the need for direct observation, however, recommending...\".","Interrupter → matching pair of commas: \"observation, however,\"."],"A, C, and D treat \"recommending…\" as if it were a full sentence."],"329255db":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", a volume of English translations of French poems,\".","What's left: \"A Sheaf Gleaned in French Fields ___ scholars' understanding\". A book title counts as one thing, so singular.","Singular → \"has enhanced\"."],"\"Poems\" and \"translations\" inside the aside are plural."],"603755a5":["B5",["Look at what follows the blank: \"that is the oldest club for Black divers... and that has helped thousands...\".","It's essential, because it tells you which club. \"That\" clauses never get a comma.","Essential \"that\" clause → no punctuation: \"club\"."],"\"That\" clauses don't take commas. D's \"and\" also breaks the pattern of \"that… and that…\"."],"8999c0c5":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"This cross-genre collaboration brought together the distinct features of Balanchine's neoclassical ballet and Graham's modern dance\". It's complete.","The blank adds a result, with no new subject.","Result add-on → comma + -ing: \"dance, showcasing\"."],"A makes a comma splice. B and D leave fragments (\"Which showcased…\" and \"Showcasing…\")."],"ad046778":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"To humans, it does not appear that the golden orb-weaver spider uses camouflage to capture its prey\" and \"the brightly colored arachnid seems to wait conspicuously...\".","\"Rather\" is a transition word, not FANBOYS, and it belongs at the start of sentence 2.","Semicolon at the boundary, then \"rather,\" → \"prey; rather,\"."],"A is a comma splice. C puts the semicolon after \"rather,\" which glues it to the wrong sentence."],"7ce4ee13":["B1",["Left side: \"In 1919 Larrazolo was elected governor of New Mexico\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"in 1928 he became the nation's first Hispanic U.S. Senator\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"New Mexico, and\"."],"B is a comma splice. A has \"and\" with no comma."],"aa443c4b":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read on: \"This hypothesis... cannot stand\". \"Cannot stand\" is the main verb.","So the blank begins a describing phrase about the hypothesis (\"suggesting that certain trees... survived...\").","Describing phrase → -ing: \"suggesting\"."],"\"Suggests\" would give \"hypothesis\" two main verbs (\"suggests… cannot stand\")."],"0a114526":["B5",["Look at \"critic Stina Chyn\". The title is stuck right onto the name, so no commas.","Look at \"claims 'expanded the range...'\". The quote blends into the sentence, so there's no comma before it either.","No punctuation anywhere → \"critic Stina Chyn claims\"."],"Commas around the name or after \"claims\" chop up a smooth sentence."],"a2293b3f":["B6",["Find the subject: \"the strings inside the instrument\".","Find its verb: \"are plucked\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation: \"instrument\"."],"\"Inside the instrument\" feels like a pause, but it's part of the subject."],"5aa1fffd":["B9",["Read the sentence: \"Where did the word 'chortle' come from?\"","Is it really asking? Yes. The passage asks it and then answers it.","Real question → question order (\"did the word... come\") + question mark."],"B and D use statement order. A has question order with a period."],"a5079e0d":["B8",["Find the two sentences: \"The measurement is based on how much and how quickly weight can be moved\" and \"one unit of mechanical horsepower is equivalent to...\".","\"For example\" is a transition phrase, not FANBOYS, and it starts sentence 2.","Semicolon at the boundary, then \"for example,\" → \"moved; for example,\"."],"A is a comma splice. B and D don't separate the two sentences."],"831b55ec":["B6",["Find the core: \"Her innovative poems popularized the use of Jamaican Creole\". \"Popularized\" is the verb and \"the use...\" is its object.","Never split a verb from its object.","No punctuation → \"popularized\"."],"A colon would need a full sentence before it. \"Her poems popularized\" isn't complete."],"42cc9236":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What does he use in a suit? \"an object\" (from \"the sound an object makes\").","\"Object\" is singular.","Singular → \"it\"."],"\"These\" is plural. \"This\" and \"that\" are vague and don't point clearly to the object."],"fa07bcb6":["B9",["Read the whole sentence: \"The clashing notes can echo a long way..., which is why ganga has been used as a communication method.\"","Is it asking? No. It's explaining why.","Statement → normal order (\"ganga has been used\") + period."],"C and D flip into question order. A adds a question mark."],"81ac953e":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", named for its creator,\".","What's left: \"The Arrhenius equation ___ an important concept\". One equation, so singular.","Singular → \"remains\"."],"The other choices are all plural."],"b32eab9f":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of Goldin, winner of the 2023 Nobel Prize in Economics for her work on women in the labor force\".","What's left: \"The trailblazing accomplishments ___ to the value\". \"Accomplishments\" is plural.","Plural → \"attest\"."],"\"Goldin\" and \"the 2023 Nobel Prize\" are singular and sit closer to the verb."],"50801257":["F4",["Find the subject: \"the International Astronomical Union\". Is there a main verb yet? No (\"naming\" at the end is an add-on).","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"Union would finally acknowledge\"."],"B, C, and D give the sentence no main verb."],"028752cd":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"During this time\", meaning the 1930s.","That's the past.","Past → \"affected\"."],"Present and future forms clash with the 1930s."],"c9a677e9":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read on: \"Aslanian's macroanalysis... shows not only...\". \"Shows\" is the main verb.","So the blank describes the macroanalysis (\"___ nearly 1,000 book titles\").","Describing phrase → -ing: \"examining\"."],"\"Examined\" or \"examines\" would give the subject two main verbs."],"1f39ab8b":["B9",["Read before the colon: \"the novel asks a central question\". So what comes after is the actual question.","A real question uses question order: \"How will Umuofia's traditions be affected\".","Question order + question mark → \"will Umuofia's traditions be affected?\"."],"A and C use statement order. B has question order with a period."],"a14eef71":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"A nearly frictionless (and... extremely slippery) state\".","What is a frictionless state? Superlubricity.","So \"superlubricity\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only D starts with it."],"A starts with \"when…,\" B with \"two surfaces,\" and C with \"reaching superlubricity.\" None of those is a state."],"5fd86f4b":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of a ghazal's couplets\" and the aside \", while adhering to the patterns of rhyme... (matla),\".","What's left: \"each one ___ thematically and logically autonomous\". \"Each one\" is singular.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Couplets,\" \"patterns,\" and \"lines\" are all plural. \"Each\" is always singular."],"f868d438":["B6",["Find the subject: \"the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek Kingelez\".","Find its verb: \"were featured\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation: \"Kingelez\"."],"A comma after the long name feels natural but splits the subject from the verb."],"60dd03bc":["B6",["Find the core: \"is home to the largest island of them all\". \"To\" is a preposition and \"the largest island\" is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"to\"."],"A dash or colon after \"to\" cuts the phrase in half."],"6d4b2e1e":["B5",["Look at \"pioneering playwright Wakako Yamauchi\". The title is stuck right onto the name.","Title + name → essential, so no commas. Then the subject runs straight into its verb, \"compares\".","No punctuation → \"playwright Wakako Yamauchi\"."],"C adds a comma between the subject and the verb."],"6205f7e4":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"is\", \"stands\", and \"are shared\" are present. The passage describes how cooperatives generally work.","It's a general fact, so present.","Present → \"benefit\"."],"Past forms make it sound like it happened once."],"7c766ceb":["F4",["Look at the verb pattern: \"ArcGIS enables cartographers... ___ maps\".","After \"enables [someone]\", English uses \"to + verb\" (\"enables them to create\").","\"to create\""],"\"Create\" and \"creating\" don't fit after \"enables cartographers.\" \"Created\" is the wrong form."],"39ac6498":["B9",["Read the sentence: \"What are atmospheric rivers, and how do they affect our weather?\"","Is it really asking? Yes. The passage asks it and then answers it.","Real question → question order (\"do they affect\") + question mark."],"B and D use statement order. A has question order with a period."],"8d00cd7c":["B6",["Find the subject: \"The roughly 200,000 km² Puntland Desert\". The size describes the desert and is part of the subject.","Find its verb: \"is classified\", right after the blank.","No punctuation inside the subject, and none before the verb → \"200,000 km² Puntland Desert\"."],"B and D split the size from the desert. A splits the subject from the verb."],"b85c19ed":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What did Stradivari make lighter? \"a traditional violin\".","\"Violin\" is singular. \"Curves\" would make no sense here.","Singular → \"it\"."],"\"Them\" would mean the curves, which doesn't make sense."],"109d5bbb":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"or FESTAC '77, as the event was more commonly known\".","Find its other end. It ends with a dash: \"known—became\".","Ends with a dash → starts with a dash. Delete test: \"the Second World Black and African Festival of Arts and Culture became the largest...\" ✓ → \"Culture—\"."],"A comma would open with a comma and close with a dash, which doesn't match."],"b5ff8f8e":["B6",["Find the core: \"the highest melting point of all the elements on the periodic table\". \"Of\" is a preposition and \"all the elements\" is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"of\"."],"A colon or dash after \"of\" cuts the phrase in half."],"505054e3":["F4",["Find the main verb. Read on past the dashes: \"a unique tradition of embroidery... remains popular\". \"Remains\" is the main verb.","So the blank begins a describing phrase about the tradition (\"___ back over two thousand years\").","Describing phrase → -ing: \"dating\"."],"\"Dates\" or \"has dated\" would give the tradition two main verbs."],"18382e67":["F2",["Whose place? Read back: \"gone are the types of pop culture references\". The personal subject takes their place.","\"Types\" is plural. Plural owner → \"their\".","\"their\""],"\"Its\" and \"it's\" are singular. \"They're\" = \"they are.\" The sculpture isn't what's being replaced."],"56770dda":["F3",["Find the time clue: \"Today\".","\"Today\" means now.","Present → \"is\"."],"\"Was\" and \"had been\" ignore \"today.\""],"3fee46f2":["B1",["Left side: \"Gitlin's use of the term referenced TV studios' practice of repackaging successful narrative formulas as new content\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"in that era, even shows that varied only slightly... still attracted sizeable audiences\". It's a full sentence, and it opens with the intro \"in that era\".","Two full sentences → semicolon, then a comma after the intro: \"content; in that era,\"."],"A and D are comma splices. C attaches \"in that era\" to the first sentence and leaves a run-on."],"6174a5b6":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", known as story starters,\".","What's left: \"Such phrases ___ from language to language\". \"Phrases\" is plural.","Plural → \"vary\"."],"\"Story starters\" is plural too, but singular \"language\" sits near the blank."],"d073983d":["B6",["Find the subject: \"Calida Garcia Rawles\". The intro already ended with a comma after \"pools\".","Find its verb: \"was\", right after the blank.","Never split a subject from its verb → no punctuation: \"Rawles\"."],"The intro ends at \"pools,\" with a comma that's already in place. Don't add another."],"02913383":["F1",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"a writing technique that ___ emphasis\".","\"That\" refers to \"technique\", which is singular.","Singular → \"creates\"."],"\"Antimetabole\" and \"technique\" are singular. Plural forms don't fit."],"e92a7ad3":["F1",["Find the subject: \"ayllus\". It's described as \"family clans\", which is plural.","Plural subject.","Plural → \"were\"."],"It's an unfamiliar word, so look at \"clans\" to see that it's plural."],"c468db1c":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"A group of ecologists... examined the defensive responses of two varieties of the sweet potato plant\". It's a full sentence.","What follows names the two varieties: \"TN57, which..., and TN66, which...\". There's no main verb.","Full sentence + names → colon: \"plant:\"."],"A and B would leave \"TN57, which… and TN66, which…\" as a fragment."],"ac7b8d04":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", an unlikely fusion of the writings of... Thoreau with today's open-world video games,\".","What's left: \"Tracy Fullerton's Walden, a game, ___ players\". One game, so singular.","Singular → \"invites\"."],"\"Writings,\" \"games,\" and \"Thoreau\" are inside the aside and pull you toward a plural verb."],"8a9d2f4e":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"of each statue's ears\" and \", like the shape of each person's ears,\".","What's left: \"the shape ___ unique\". \"Shape\" is singular.","Singular → \"is\"."],"\"Ears\" is plural and right before the verb. That's the trap."],"576b2c70":["F4",["The blank is inside a \"which\" clause: \"Planetary Flight Handbook, which ___ detailed mathematical guidance\".","A \"which\" clause needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"provided\"."],"\"Having provided,\" \"to provide,\" and \"providing\" aren't real verbs."],"78e978b5":["B5",["Look at \"fellow OECD nations Chile and Luxembourg\". The descriptor runs right into the names.","Label + names → essential, so no punctuation (the names tell you which nations).","No punctuation → \"nations Chile\"."],"A colon or comma would split the label from the names it describes."],"d4fe8f03":["B5",["Look at \"US artists Thomas Hart Benton and Jackson Pollock\". The descriptor runs right into the names.","Label + names → essential, so no commas. Two names joined by \"and\" → no comma either.","No punctuation → \"artists Thomas Hart Benton and Jackson Pollock\"."],"A's colon has no full sentence before it. C puts a comma before \"and\" in a two-name pair."],"dfbf5d33":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"Upon recovering two years later\".","Who recovered? Henry (the king who fell ill).","So \"Henry\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only A starts with him."],"B and C start with \"reign,\" and a reign doesn't recover from illness. D starts with \"it.\""],"cf08e2fd":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"From 1912 to 1951\", \"owned\", \"operated\", \"was\" are all past.","It's the same past period.","Past → \"became\"."],"\"Will become\" and \"is becoming\" don't fit events from 1912–1951."],"bb4557cf":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"The relationship between genomes and epigenomes reveals how cells with identical DNA develop different functions\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains how: \"whereas the genome... contains..., the epigenome consists of...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"functions:\"."],"B is a comma splice. A is a run-on."],"da53d726":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"The conference's goals were threefold\". It's a full sentence.","What follows is the list of three goals: \"to ensure..., to secure..., and to advocate...\".","Full sentence + list → colon: \"threefold: to\"."],"B and D would leave \"To ensure…, to secure…, and to advocate…\" as a fragment."],"f2eaaf5d":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"a country in Western Asia\".","Find its other end. It ends with a dash: \"Asia—is\".","Ends with a dash → starts with a dash. Delete test: \"the preamble to the constitution of Bahrain is much longer\" ✓ → \"Bahrain—a\"."],"A comma would open with a comma and close with a dash, which doesn't match."],"ec08463d":["F4",["Find the subject: \"One species, the silverleaf sunflower,\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"includes\"."],"\"Having included,\" \"including,\" and \"to include\" leave no main verb."],"0b005ae2":["B2",["Left side: \"...unearthed Neronian tools... may provide evidence that interactions... occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously supposed\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"a finding that, if true, would overturn current theories...\". The verb belongs to \"that\", so there's no main verb. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → just a comma: \"supposed, a\"."],"A and B would leave \"A finding that…\" alone as a fragment."],"4a90a978":["F4",["Find the main verb: \"Ellen Ochoa left her position\". The sentence already has it.","The blank explains why she left (the purpose).","Purpose → \"to join\"."],"\"Joined\" or \"joins\" would be a second main verb with no \"and.\""],"59e41600":["F4",["Find the subject: \"Scientists\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"explain\"."],"\"Having explained,\" \"explaining,\" and \"to explain\" leave no main verb."],"eb03096e":["B6",["Find the core: \"embodied by Native Hawaiians\". \"By\" is a preposition and \"Native Hawaiians\" is its object.","Never split a preposition from its object.","No punctuation → \"by\"."],"Any mark after \"by\" cuts the phrase."],"d75d57a0":["B2",["Left side: \"While many video game creators strive to make their graphics ever more lifelike\". It starts with \"While\", so it can't stand alone. It's a fragment.","Right side: \"others look to the past, developing titles...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma (\"while\" already shows the contrast, so no \"but\"): \"lifelike,\"."],"D's \", but\" treats the \"while…\" clause like a full sentence. \"While\" already shows the contrast."],"a427a52c":["B2",["Left side: \"Powered with energy collected by solar panels during the day\". There's no subject or main verb. It's an intro fragment, and it should stay in one piece.","Right side: \"the blinking LEDs keep lions away at night\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → one comma, at the end of the intro: \"energy collected by solar panels during the day,\"."],"A and D chop up the intro with extra commas. B has no comma before the main sentence."],"6997261f":["B6",["Find the core: \"the oldest pieces of pottery\". \"Of pottery\" tells you what the pieces are.","Never split a noun from the \"of\" phrase that finishes it.","No punctuation → \"pieces\"."],"A dash or colon would split \"pieces\" from what they're pieces of."],"3daf126e":["F4",["The blank is inside a \"that\" clause: \"predicts that the rate of speciation... on an isolated island... ___ triple the rate...\".","A \"that\" clause needs a real verb.","Real verb → \"will be\"."],"\"Being,\" \"to be,\" and \"to have been\" aren't real verbs."],"975eda7c":["F4",["Find the subject: \"people in the Americas\". Is there a main verb yet? No.","The sentence needs a real verb. \"For thousands of years\" (up to now) points to has/have.","Real verb → \"have used\"."],"\"To use,\" \"having used,\" and \"using\" leave no main verb."],"5ee7fb04":["B1",["Left side: \"In modern plays, actors typically won't acknowledge the audience\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"To do so breaks the fourth wall...\". \"To do so\" is the subject and \"breaks\" is the verb, so it's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"audience. To\"."],"D is a comma splice. A's \"As to do so\" makes no sense."],"a12e3b8a":["B1",["Left side: \"This idea is known as the service recovery paradox\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"research suggests that it has important implications for customer loyalty...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS (comma before \"and\"): \"paradox, and\"."],"A is a comma splice. D puts the comma after \"and.\""],"c04e9136":["B7",["Spot the list of three pioneers: Barry Sharpless, who coined...; Carolyn Bertozzi, founder of the Bertozzi Group at Stanford; and Morten Meldal, a professor....","Do the items have commas inside? Yes. And there's already a semicolon after item 1.","Complex list → semicolon after item 2: \"Stanford;\"."],"A comma would blur where Bertozzi's description ends and Meldal begins."],"588887b9":["F3",["Find the timeline: Faure redesigned the battery in 1881 (past). Planté invented it fifteen years before that.","A past event that happened before another past event uses \"had\".","Earlier past → \"had invented\"."],"Present and future forms ignore that this happened before 1881."],"92f309b2":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"a statue... was erected in the place that inspired the song's name\". It's a full sentence.","What follows names that place: \"Gangnam District in Seoul, South Korea\". There's no verb.","Full sentence + name → a single dash (it works like a colon): \"name—\"."],"A semicolon or period would leave \"Gangnam District in Seoul…\" as a fragment."],"c49e946e":["B7",["Spot the list of three responsibilities: overseeing the... collections, which boast more than 162 million items / managing the US Copyright Office, which... / appointing the US poet laureate.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes. And there's a \"; and\" later.","Complex list → semicolon between items: \"items; managing\"."],"B uses a comma, which blurs the items. D's period breaks up the list."],"6d247c13":["F4",["Look at the main sentence: \"the saloon was known to offer elegant accommodations\". It's complete.","The opening \"___ by businessman William A.G. Brown\" describes the saloon. It was created by him.","Describing phrase, done to the saloon → -ed form: \"Created\"."],"\"Creating\" would mean the saloon did the creating. \"Creates\" and \"create\" are main verbs."],"fe41f258":["B2",["Left side: \"Epicurus defined pleasure as 'the absence of pain in the body and of trouble in the soul'\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"positing that all life's virtues derived from this absence\". It's an -ing phrase with no subject. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → comma (inside the quote mark): \"soul,” positing\"."],"B, C, and D treat \"positing…\" like a full sentence."],"4c06427b":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \", like neuroscience and biophysics expert Elba Serrano,\".","What's left: \"many of whom ___ this mechanism\". \"Many\" is plural.","Plural → \"study\"."],"Elba Serrano is one person and sits right before the blank. That's the trap."],"81f09e07":["F4",["Find the main clause: \"ancient Egyptians slept on hard, elevated headrests\". It's complete.","After the comma, \"their design ___ airflow...\" is a describing add-on, not a second sentence.","Describing add-on → -ing: \"prioritizing\"."],"\"Prioritized\" or \"was prioritizing\" would make \"their design…\" a second sentence spliced on with a comma."],"8790d061":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"a construction system known as falsework\".","Find its other end. It starts with the comma after \"supports\".","Starts with a comma → ends with a comma. Delete test: \"These temporary supports were removed when the bridge was completed\" ✓ → \"falsework,\"."],"A semicolon or colon wouldn't match the opening comma."],"8a264a54":["B2",["Left side: \"With a blend of traditional design elements, such as arched Gothic ceilings, and modern ones, such as floor-to-ceiling windows\". There's no subject or main verb. It's an intro fragment.","Right side: \"transitional design splits the difference between old and new...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"windows, transitional\"."],"A and C treat the intro like a full sentence."],"9902d2de":["B1",["Left side: \"The Alvarez theory... maintained that the secondary effects of an asteroid impact caused many dinosaurs... to die out\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"it left unexplored the question of whether... volcanic activity might have also contributed\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → comma + FANBOYS: \"out, but\"."],"D is a comma splice. A has \"but\" with no comma."],"1c65db8f":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"the organism uses to protect itself from predation\".","What's left: \"the toxins... actually ___ its attractiveness\". \"Toxins\" is plural.","Plural → \"increase\"."],"\"Organism\" is singular and sits right before the verb."],"cd2443c0":["F5",["Read the opening phrase: \"A pioneer in the field of taphonomy (the study of how organic remains become fossils)\".","Who is a pioneer? Anna Behrensmeyer.","So \"Anna Behrensmeyer\" has to be the first thing after the comma. Only B starts with her."],"A starts with \"fossils,\" C with \"it,\" and D with \"a 2005 analysis.\" None of those is a pioneer."],"e44db0a0":["F1",["Find the subject: \"a cycle of lunar phases\".","The thing doing the taking is \"cycle\", which is singular. \"Phases\" is just in the \"of\" phrase.","Singular → \"takes\"."],"\"Phases\" is plural and right before the verb."],"64a40675":["B6",["Find the core: \"he preferred the sound of a bent bell\". \"Preferred\" is the verb and \"the sound\" is its object.","Never split a verb from its object.","No punctuation → \"preferred the\"."],"Any mark after \"preferred\" cuts it off from what he preferred."],"b8e13a74":["F1",["Find the subject. Cross out \"exhibited by Marcel Duchamp\" and the dash aside \"—one of several works that received scorn from critics—\".","What's left: \"an abstract cubist painting ___ the Western art canon\". One painting, so singular.","Singular → \"has entered\"."],"\"Works\" and \"critics\" in the aside are plural."],"265e7f4c":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"In 1613\" and \"ignited\" (past).","It's the same past event.","Past → \"malfunctioned\"."],"Present and future forms break the past-tense story."],"d222f7d9":["B4",["Spot the extra info: \"widely understood to confer stability and efficiency in the locomotion of a variety of microscopic organisms—including bacteria, eukaryotic algae, and ciliates\".","Find its other end. It ends with the dash after \"ciliates\", and then the main verb \"bestows\" comes. So it opens with a comma after \"Helical swimming\", and the blank gets no verb.","Delete test: \"Helical swimming bestows similar...\" ✓ → \"swimming,\"."],"A, B, and C add a verb (\"is\" or \"has been\"), which gives \"helical swimming\" two main verbs."],"4b424102":["B1",["Left side: \"The Balkan nation of Bosnia and Herzegovina does not have one president but three\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"Known as members of the office of the presidency..., these elected officials take turns...\". It's a full sentence.","Two full sentences → period: \"three. Known\"."],"A and D are comma splices. \"Known…\" describes the second sentence's subject, \"these officials.\""],"96e5da01":["F3",["Find the time clues: \"might be used... to build future... systems\". These systems don't exist yet. You're describing what they would do in general.","For what something does in general, use simple present.","Present → \"produce\"."],"\"Produced\" and \"have produced\" refer to the past, but these systems don't exist yet."],"2c4cd76d":["B3",["Read before the blank: \"Researchers... have determined why some soil-dwelling roundworms... move in the opposite direction... when searching for food\". It's a full sentence.","What follows explains why: \"in the Northern Hemisphere, the magnetic field points down..., but in the Southern Hemisphere, it points up...\".","Full sentence + explanation → colon: \"food:\"."],"B is a comma splice. D is a run-on."],"12bd5b75":["F2",["What does the blank refer to? What was built over forty years? \"the Salt Lake Temple\".","One temple → singular.","Singular → \"it was\"."],"\"They,\" \"both,\" and \"one\" don't match a single temple."],"3ed5ebb4":["B2",["Left side: \"scholar Candace Waid observes that the novel depicts the upper classes... as 'consumed by the appetite of a soulless materialism'\". It's a full sentence.","Right side: \"an apt assessment given that The House of Mirth is set during the Gilded Age...\". There's no main verb. It's a fragment.","Sentence + fragment → comma (inside the quote mark): \"materialism,”\"."],"A and B add \"and,\" which treats the add-on like a full sentence."],"67614549":["F3",["Find the time clues: Lincoln appointed his cabinet in 1860, and \"was surprising\" and \"had run\" are past.","It's a past action.","Past → \"leveraged\"."],"\"Has leveraged\" and \"is leveraging\" make it sound ongoing."],"78b88c04":["B7",["Spot the list of three things: Chickasaw Basic, in 2009 / an online television network, Chickasaw TV, in 2010 / a Rosetta Stone course..., in 2015.","Do the items have commas inside? Yes. Each item ends with \", in [year]\". And there's a \"; and\" later.","Year stays in its item with a comma, semicolon after the year → \"Basic, in 2009; an online television network,\"."],"A and B put the semicolon before \"in 2009,\" which pulls the year into the wrong item."],"d1482e27":["F1",["Find the subject: \"The map\". The other verbs, \"shows\" and \"includes\", are singular too.","One map → singular.","Singular → \"describes\"."],"\"Describe,\" \"have described,\" and \"are describing\" are plural."],"9f0ac61d":["B2",["Left side: \"Hoping to help her students and their families outside the classroom\". There's no subject or main verb. It's an intro fragment.","Right side: \"Huerta left teaching to start the Stockton chapter...\". It's a full sentence.","Fragment + sentence → just a comma: \"classroom,\"."],"A period or semicolon would leave \"Hoping…\" as a fragment."],"bd11fe93":["F4",["Find the main verb: \"She uses chemistry and fossil evidence\". The sentence already has it.","The blank explains why she uses them (the purpose).","Purpose → \"to study\"."],"\"Studies\" or \"studied\" would be a second main verb with no \"and.\""],"f570cece":["F6",["How many towns? Two (Arteaga and Lagos de Moreno). Do they own something? Yes, the natural beauty and traditions.","Several owners → s'.","\"towns'\""],"\"Town's\" means only one town. \"Towns\" drops the ownership."]};